var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"cont_func/#Manipulating-Contribution-Functions","page":"Contribution Functions","text":"Formation temperatures are a summary statistic of the contribution functions, which FormTempResult also carries in its cont_func field. To plot them:\n\nusing Korg\nusing PythonPlot; plt = PythonPlot.pyplot\nusing FormationTemps; FT = FormationTemps\nplt.style.use(joinpath(FT.moddir, \"fig.mplstyle\"))\n\n# get the linelist\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[16000:16100]\n\n# set stellar parameters\nTeff = 5777.0\nlogg = 4.44\nFe_H = 0.0\nvsini = 2100.0\nζ_RT = 3400.0   # radial-tangential macroturbulent broadening\nξ = 850.0       # microturbulent broadening\n\n# create StellarProps composite type to hold everything\nstar_props = StellarProps(Teff=Teff, logg=logg, Fe_H=Fe_H,\n                          vsini=vsini, v_macro=ζ_RT, v_micro=ξ)\n\n# get the flux + formation temperature spectra\nform_temp_result = FT.calc_formation_temp(star_props, linelist; Δλ=0.005)\n\n# parse the result\nwavs = form_temp_result.wavs\nflux = form_temp_result.flux\ntemp = form_temp_result.form_temps\ncont_func = form_temp_result.cont_func\n\n# cont_func holds per-interval integrals, so its magnitude follows the layer spacing of the\n# model atmosphere. Convert to a density per dex of reference optical depth before plotting\n# against depth; see the note below on integrals vs densities.\ncont_dens = cfunc_per_dex(form_temp_result)\n\n# plot the output\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6,4.8))\nimg = ax1.pcolormesh(cont_dens ./ maximum(cont_dens), cmap=\"viridis\")\nax1.set_xticklabels([])\nax1.set_yticklabels([])\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"cont_func_simple_example.png\")\ncbar = fig.colorbar(img, ax=ax1)\ncbar.set_label(\"{\\\\rm Normalized Flux Contribution Fn.}\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: formation_temps)","title":"Manipulating Contribution Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"cont_func/#Integrals-or-densities?","page":"Contribution Functions","text":"Element [k, j] of cont_func is the contribution of the atmosphere interval between layers k and k+1 to the flux at wavelength j. Summing a column gives the emergent flux, in the units of Korg.synthesize and on the same scale for every method; result.flux is that sum divided by the continuum sum:\n\nsum(result.cont_func, dims=1)   # the emergent flux\n\nEach element already includes the width of its interval, so:\n\nFor sums over depth, use cont_func as it is. Weighted means, cumulative distributions and the 50% crossing behind form_temps_from_cfunc are all of this kind.\nFor comparisons across depth, such as plotting against depth or taking a ratio of two intervals, convert to a density with cfunc_per_dex, which gives dF/dlog₁₀τ_ref. MARCS layers are 0.1 dex apart in log τref between −3 and +1 and 0.2 dex outside, so raw ```contfuncjumps by a factor of two at those depths for no physical reason. The plots on this page use the density, andceilingratio``converts internally, which is why it needsτref`.\n\nTo label the depth axis, take the atmosphere structure from the result:\n\n\n# parse the atmosphere\natm = form_temp_result.atmosphere\nzs = get_zs(atm)\nTs = get_Ts(atm)\nτs = get_τs(atm)\n\n# plot temperature structure vs optical depth (if available) or height\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6,4.8))\nif isempty(τs)\n    ax1.plot(zs ./ 1e5, Ts, c=\"k\")   # fall back to height in km\n    ax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Height [km]}\")\nelse\n    ax1.plot(τs, Ts, c=\"k\")\n    ax1.set_xscale(\"log\")\n    ax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Optical Depth at 5000 \\\\AA}\")\nend\nax1.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Atmosphere Temperature [K]}\")\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"atmosphere.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: formation_temps)\n\nZoomed in on a few lines, with the depth axis labeled:\n\n\n# choose some lines to zoom into\nlines_to_focus = linelist[5:25]\nrest_wls = [1e8 * l.wl for l in lines_to_focus]\nbuffer = 0.5\nidx1 = findfirst(x -> x .>= minimum(rest_wls) - buffer, wavs)\nidx2 = findfirst(x -> x .>= maximum(rest_wls) + buffer, wavs)\n\n# pixel centers for pcolormesh: one per wavelength bin, and the log-τ midpoint of each\n# layer interval (cont_dens is a density per dex, so its rows are centered in log τ)\nxcenters = view(wavs, idx1:idx2)\nycenters = elav(log10.(τs))\ncfuncp = view(cont_dens, :, idx1:idx2)\n\n# plot the contribution function\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6,4.8))\nimg = ax1.pcolormesh(xcenters, ycenters, cfuncp ./ maximum(cfuncp), cmap=\"viridis\")\nax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Vacuum Wavelength [\\\\AA]}\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"\\$\\\\log_{10}\\\\,\\\\tau\\$ {\\\\rm at 5000 \\\\AA}\")\ncbar = fig.colorbar(img, ax=ax1)\ncbar.set_label(\"{\\\\rm Normalized Flux Contribution Fn.}\")\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"cont_func_example.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: formation_temps)","title":"Integrals or densities?"},{"category":"section","location":"cont_func/#Formation-temperatures-can-lie-to-you!","page":"Contribution Functions","text":"Pixels with the same formation temperature need not have similar contribution functions. In each frame below, the black pixels in the line cores share a formation temperature to within a couple of Kelvin, yet their contribution functions differ markedly. They converge toward the continuum, but most of the radial-velocity information sits in the steep line flanks, where they do not. Sections 4.2 and 4.3 of the paper presenting FormationTemps.jl discuss this further; read them before relying on formation temperatures.\n\n(Image: line_animation) (Image: cont_func_animation)","title":"Formation temperatures can lie to you!"},{"category":"section","location":"cont_func/#Lines-the-model-cannot-get-right","page":"Contribution Functions","text":"boundary_mask only catches lines whose contribution function has not decayed by the top of the model atmosphere. It cannot catch a line whose physics is wrong: Korg assumes LTE and MARCS has no chromosphere, so a chromospheric line, a line far from LTE, or one with poor atomic data gives a well-behaved contribution function and a meaningless formation temperature.\n\nThe repo carries a curated list of such lines in data/bad_lines.csv, applied by scripts/bad_lines_mask.jl. Each row gives an air wavelength (lambda_air), a species and a reason: chromo, nlte or linedata.\n\nusing FormationTemps; FT = FormationTemps\ninclude(joinpath(FT.moddir, \"scripts\", \"bad_lines_mask.jl\"))\n\nentries = read_bad_lines(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"bad_lines.csv\");\n                         vacuum=true, max_extent_default=MAX_EXTENT_DEFAULT)\nentries = verify_species_present(entries, line_wavs, line_species;\n                                 n_sigma_halo=N_SIGMA_HALO, v_broad=v_broad)\n\nlm = build_line_mask(result.wavs, result.flux, entries;\n                     n_sigma_halo=N_SIGMA_HALO, depth_thresh=DEPTH_THRESH,\n                     min_core_depth=MIN_CORE_DEPTH, v_broad=v_broad,\n                     core_frac=CORE_FRAC)\n\nline_wavs and line_species come from the linelist the synthesis used, and v_broad = sqrt(vsini^2 + ζ^2 + ξ^2).\n\nnote: Packages the script loads\nThe script loads CSV, DataFrames and Korg. Outside the FormationTemps project, add them to your environment first:using Pkg; Pkg.add([\"CSV\", \"DataFrames\", \"Korg\"])\n\nThe line mask uses bits 0x02, 0x04 and 0x08 for the three reasons and leaves 0x01 for the boundary flag, so the two combine into one array:\n\nbnd = ifelse.(boundary_mask(result), MASK_BOUNDARY, 0x00)\nmask = lm.mask .| bnd\ngood = mask .== 0x00\n\nEach entry masks the line's own core: build_line_mask starts at the flux minimum near the listed wavelength and widens until the line rises back above max(DEPTH_THRESH, CORE_FRAC · core_depth). report_line_mask prints the region each entry settled on.\n\nverify_species_present drops, with a warning, any entry whose species has no nearby line in the linelist. Without it, an entry for a missing transition would mask whatever unrelated line happened to be deepest nearby.\n\nThis is a script, not package API: the list is data you can inspect and amend.","title":"Lines the model cannot get right"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Public-Functions","page":"Public Functions","text":"The public functions exported by FormationTemps.jl are documented on this page. The high-level convenience functions  should meet the needs of most users. However, some potentially useful, slightly lower-level methods are exposed by FormationTemps.jl and documented below. A full index of all methods defined by FormationTemps.jl is available in the Full Index.","title":"Public Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#High-level-Convenience-Functions","page":"Public Functions","text":"FormationTemps.jl provides a few high-level convenience wrappers to produce flux spectra and formation temperatures.","title":"High-level Convenience Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Atmosphere-Types","page":"Public Functions","text":"FormTempResult includes an atmosphere field which contains the model atmosphere used in the spectrum modeling. ","title":"Atmosphere Types"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Empirical-Relations","page":"Public Functions","text":"A few works have reported empirical relationships between fundamental stellar parameters and the measured micro- and macroturbulent velocities. FormationTemps.jl implements these for cases where micro- and macroturbulence are not available. ","title":"Empirical Relations"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Resolution-Degradation-LSF-Modeling","page":"Public Functions","text":"Spectra are synthesized at infinite spectral resolving power (but finite sampling). Since instruments imprint some LSF on spectra, model spectra are generally convolved with model LSFs. Functions for performing these operations are documented here. ","title":"Resolution Degradation + LSF Modeling"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Convolutions-and-Kernels","page":"Public Functions","text":"Convolution operations are often used in the modeling of spectra, even when explicit disk integration is performed (e.g., for microturbulent broadening). FormationTemps.jl exposes these convolution methods.\n\nThe broadening kernels (which are evaluated internally in the above convolution functions) can also be directly calculated.","title":"Convolutions & Kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Contribution-Function-Diagnostics","page":"Public Functions","text":"A formation temperature is only meaningful where the flux contribution has decayed before the model atmosphere is truncated. ceiling_ratio measures how far from that condition each wavelength is, and boundary_mask thresholds it. calc_formation_temp warns on the same statistic at the BOUNDARY_R_THRESH default, so its count matches boundary_mask(result) unless you pass a different r_thresh.\n\nBoth compare the topmost layer interval against the column peak, so both need the reference optical depth grid to divide out the interval width; see Integrals or densities? for why cont_func cannot be reduced across depth as it stands.\n\nThese diagnose the integration domain only. For lines that are untrustworthy on physical grounds despite a well-behaved contribution function, the repo carries a curated list outside the package API; see Lines the model cannot get right.","title":"Contribution Function Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Absorption-Cache","page":"Public Functions","text":"AlphaCache provides a reusable cache that accelerates repeated compute_alpha! calls by warm-starting the electron density solver and reusing continuum buffers across calls.","title":"Absorption Cache"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Miscellaneous-Utilities","page":"Public Functions","text":"","title":"Miscellaneous Utilities"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp","page":"Public Functions","text":"calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; use_gpu=GPU_DEFAULT, Δλ=0.01,\n                    gpu_precision=Float64, method=:disk,\n                    minλ=NaN, maxλ=NaN, buffer=2.0, u1=NaN, u2=NaN,\n                    Nϕ=128, Nμ=32, N_az=256,\n                    r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH,\n                    showprogress=true, kwargs...)\n\nCompute flux formation temperatures, normalized flux, and flux contribution function for a given star and linelist.\n\nThe wavelength grid is built from the line list (vacuum cm → Å) with padding and step Δλ. Use minλ/maxλ (Angstrom) to override the default bounds (first/last line ± 2 A).\n\nReturns a FormTempResult with fields:\n\nwavs: wavelength grid (Angstrom).\nflux: normalized flux (sum(cfunc_dt_flux) / sum(cfunc_dt_flux_cont)).\nform_temps: formation temperature defined at 50% of the cumulative flux contribution.\ncont_func: contribution function, shape (Natm - 1, Nλ): each interval's contribution to the emergent surface flux F = 2π∫Iμdμ, in the units of Korg.synthesize's flux and on the same scale for every method.\nceiling_ratio: per-wavelength top-of-atmosphere contamination statistic; see ceiling_ratio.\nr_thresh: the contamination threshold used, echoing the r_thresh keyword.\natmosphere: atmosphere structure used for the calculation.\n\nwarning: Formation temperatures can be contaminated by the top of the atmosphere\nWhere the flux contribution is still peaking at the top of the model, form_temps is set by where the model was truncated rather than by the line, and is a lower limit. Expect this in strong saturated cores. result.ceiling_ratio quantifies it per wavelength and boundary_mask flags the affected wavelengths, using the r_thresh recorded on the result so the mask matches what the calculation warned about. r_thresh moves both.\n\nDisk-integration method\n\nmethod selects how the stellar disk is integrated; see the \"Integration Methods\" guide for accuracy and speed trade-offs.\n\n:disk (default) — explicit integration over Nϕ latitude bins. The reference: self-consistent limb darkening, inclination istar, differential rotation (α₂, α₄).\n:quadrature — ring-by-ring Gauss–Legendre μ-quadrature (Nμ nodes, N_az azimuth samples for the per-ring Doppler kernel). Reproduces the :disk physics at a fraction of the cost, solving transfer once per μ node instead of once per tile.\n:hirano — analytic Hirano et al. (2011) rotation + macroturbulence convolution using quadratic limb-darkening coefficients u1, u2. Cheapest, but assumes a parametric limb darkening law and no center-to-limb line-profile variation.\n\nconvolve is a deprecated alias: false ⇒ :disk, true ⇒ :hirano.\n\nAll three methods support use_gpu=true. showprogress=false suppresses the disk integration progress bar.\n\ngpu_precision=Float32 runs GPU computations at single precision, roughly halving GPU memory use. Absorption coefficients are always Float64 (a Korg requirement) and converted before upload. Keep the Float64 default for :quadrature, which loses accuracy at Float32.\n\nThe CPU :disk path is parallelized across tiles with Threads.@threads; launch Julia with multiple threads (julia -t auto) to benefit. See Parallelization.\n\nHydrogen (Balmer/Brackett) lines are included by default, since Korg computes them from dedicated Stark/MHD physics rather than from the linelist. hydrogen_lines=false, hydrogen_line_window_size_Å and use_MHD are forwarded to compute_alpha!.\n\nExamples\n\nstar = StellarProps(Teff=5777.0, logg=4.44, Fe_H=0.0, vsini=2100.0)\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[1:500]\n\n# explicit disk integration (default)\nresult = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01)\n\n# ring-by-ring μ-quadrature\nresult_q = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:quadrature)\n\n# analytic Hirano convolution (needs limb-darkening coefficients)\nresult_h = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:hirano, u1=0.43, u2=0.31)\n\n# Float32 GPU:\nresult32 = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, gpu_precision=Float32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp_chunked","page":"Public Functions","text":"calc_formation_temp_chunked(star, linelist; chunk_width=50.0, wing_padding=30.0,\n                             overlap=5.0, Δλ=0.001, buffer=2.0,\n                             callback=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nCompute formation temperatures over an arbitrarily large wavelength range by processing fixed-width wavelength chunks.\n\nEach chunk selects lines within [λ_start - wing_padding, λ_stop + wing_padding], ensuring that broad line wings contribute correctly at chunk boundaries.\n\nArguments\n\nchunk_width: width of each wavelength chunk in Å.\nwing_padding: extra range (Å) beyond each chunk edge for linelist selection. 30 Å is conservative enough for the strongest lines (H-alpha, Ca II).\noverlap: overlap width (Å) between adjacent chunks for smooth stitching.\ncallback: optional (chunk_idx, result::FormTempResult, ll_chunk) -> nothing called after each chunk, where ll_chunk is the padded linelist view used for that chunk.  When provided, results are not accumulated and the function returns nothing.  Use this for streaming to disk when the full result is too large for RAM.\nAll other keyword arguments are forwarded to calc_formation_temp.\n\nWhen callback=nothing, returns a Vector{FormTempResult} with one entry per chunk.  Stitching is the caller's responsibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp_chunked"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.StellarProps","page":"Public Functions","text":"StellarProps(; Teff=NaN, logg=NaN, Fe_H=NaN, vsini=0.0, v_macro=NaN, v_micro=NaN,\n              ρstar=1.0, istar=90.0, α₂=0.0, α₄=0.0)\n\nContainer for stellar parameters and broadening properties used by calc_formation_temp.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nTeff: effective temperature (K).\nlogg: log10 surface gravity (cgs).\nFe_H: metallicity [Fe/H] (dex); used to build the abundance vector A_X.\nvsini: projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nv_macro: macroturbulent velocity scale ζ (m/s); if NaN, uses vmac_fit(Teff, logg).\nv_micro: microturbulent velocity ξ (m/s). Scalar for uniform broadening; vector of length Natm for per-layer broadening. If scalar NaN, uses vmic_fit(Teff).\nρstar: stellar radius scale factor for disk integration (dimensionless; default 1). No effect on any output: cont_func is a flux per unit surface area, and the rotation field depends only on x_sky/ρstar.\nistar: stellar inclination (degrees; 90 = equator-on). No physical effect for rigid rotation (α₂=α₄=0), where v_los = -vsini·x_sky/ρstar and the broadening follows from the projected vsini alone. It matters once differential rotation is on, where it selects which latitude bands are visible and how they are weighted. Numerically it is an exact no-op for method=:quadrature; for method=:disk at finite Nϕ it changes which tiles are visible, shifting the result at the discretization level.\nα₂, α₄: differential-rotation coefficients in the normalized rate law Ω(ϕ)/Ω_eq = f(ϕ) = 1 - α₂·sin²ϕ - α₄·sin⁴ϕ. Default 0 (solid body); positive values make the equator rotate faster than the poles. vsini stays the projected equatorial velocity (f(0)=1).\n\nStruct fields:\n\nTeff, logg, Fe_H, A_X: atmosphere parameters (A_X is the full abundance vector).\nvsini, ζ, ξ: rotational, macroturbulent, and microturbulent velocities (m/s). ξ is T (scalar) or AbstractVector{T} (per-layer).\nρstar, istar, α₂, α₄: disk integration parameters (α₂, α₄ are the differential-rotation coefficients).\n\nSee also: vmac_fit, vmic_fit, calc_formation_temp\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.StellarProps"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.FormTempResult","page":"Public Functions","text":"FormTempResult(wavs, flux, form_temps, cont_func, atmosphere; r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH)\nFormTempResult(wavs, flux, form_temps, cont_func, ceiling_ratio, r_thresh, atmosphere)\n\nContainer for calc_formation_temp outputs.\n\nFields:\n\nwavs: wavelength grid (Angstrom).\nflux: normalized flux across the grid.\nform_temps: formation temperature (K) at cumulative flux contribution of 0.5.\ncont_func: contribution function as per-interval integrals (C × Δτ_λ), size (Natm - 1, Nλ). sum(cont_func, dims=1) is the emergent surface flux F = 2π∫Iμdμ, so each element carries its own layer width. Units are those of Korg.synthesize's flux, on the same scale for every method. Sums over depth (weights, cumulative distributions) use it directly and are independent of the layer grid; plotting it against depth, or comparing one interval against another, requires cfunc_per_dex first.\nceiling_ratio: per-wavelength top-of-atmosphere contamination statistic; see ceiling_ratio.\nr_thresh: the contamination threshold this result was computed with. boundary_mask defaults to it, so the mask matches the wavelengths the calculation warned about.\natmosphere: atmosphere structure used for the calculation.\n\nThe five-argument form derives ceiling_ratio from cont_func and the atmosphere's τ_ref, and is what the internal paths use; prefer it over the seven-argument form, which cannot enforce that the two agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.FormTempResult"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.Atmosphere","page":"Public Functions","text":"Atmosphere{T}\n\nAbstract atmosphere type used by formation temperature calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.Atmosphere"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.get_τs","page":"Public Functions","text":"get_τs(atm)\n\nReturn the optical depth reference grid as a standard Array. Returns an empty vector when the atmosphere was constructed from a model that does not supply tau_ref (in that case the Bézier τ integrator is used and atm.τs is empty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_τs"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.get_zs","page":"Public Functions","text":"get_zs(atm)\n\nReturn the height grid as a standard Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_zs"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.get_Ts","page":"Public Functions","text":"get_Ts(atm)\n\nReturn the temperature grid as a standard Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_Ts"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.vmac_fit","page":"Public Functions","text":"vmac_fit(teff, logg)\nvmac_fit(teff)\n\nEmpirical macroturbulent velocity fits.\n\nvmac_fit(teff, logg) uses the Doyle et al. (2014) relation with teff in K and logg in cgs. vmac_fit(teff) uses the Bruntt et al. (2010) relation with teff in K. Both return the macroturbulent velocity in m/s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.vmac_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.vmic_fit","page":"Public Functions","text":"vmic_fit(teff)\n\nEmpirical microturbulent velocity fit from Bruntt et al. (2010).\n\nInput teff is in K; returns microturbulent velocity in m/s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.vmic_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.convolve_instrument_gauss","page":"Public Functions","text":"convolve_instrument_gauss(xs, ys; new_res=1.17e5, oversampling=2.0)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with a Gaussian LSF at resolving power new_res.  The output spectrum is resampled onto a new wavelength grid (evenly spaced in log-wavelength, i.e., velocity)  at the specified oversampling factor. \n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Input wavelength grid.\nys::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Input spectrum sampled on xs.\nnew_res::Real=1.17e5: Target resolving power (λ/Δλ) for the Gaussian LSF.\noversampling::Real=2.0: Oversampling factor for the output log-wavelength grid.\n\nReturns:\n\nxs_out::Vector{<:Real}: Output wavelength grid at the requested resolution.\nys_out::Vector{<:Real}: Spectrum convolved with the Gaussian LSF and rebinned to xs_out.\n\nSee also: rebin_spectrum\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_instrument_gauss"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.rebin_spectrum","page":"Public Functions","text":"rebin_spectrum(xs_old, ys_old, xs_new)\nrebin_spectrum(xs_old, ys_old, σs_old, xs_new)\n\nRebin a spectrum (and optional uncertainties) from xs_old to xs_new.\n\nBased on the SpectRes spectral resampling implementation: https://github.com/ACCarnall/SpectRes/blob/master/spectres/spectral_resampling.py\n\nNote: the algorithm may introduce small shifts if the wavelength grids are misaligned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.rebin_spectrum"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation","page":"Public Functions","text":"convolve_gray_rotation(xs, ys, vsini, u1)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the Gray (2008) rotation kernel using linear limb darkening.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum with the same shape as ys.\n\nSee also: gray_rot_kernel, convolve_gray_rotation_gpu\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro","page":"Public Functions","text":"convolve_hirano_rotmacro(xs, ys, vsini, ζ_rt, u1, u2; intres=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the Hirano et al. (2011) combined rotation+macroturbulence kernel. The kernel is computed analytically in the Fourier domain and applied via FFT.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nu2::Real: Quadratic limb-darkening coefficient.\nintres::Int=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS: Number of quadrature points for the disk integral.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum with the same shape as ys.\n\nSee also: hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel, convolve_hirano_rotmacro_gpu\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro","page":"Public Functions","text":"convolve_iso_rt_macro(xs, ys, ζ_rt)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the isotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.8). This is the disk-integrated (μ-averaged) form appropriate for stellar flux spectra.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nζ_rt::Real: Isotropic macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum (or ys if ζ_rt == 0).\n\nSee also: gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel, convolve_iso_rt_macro_gpu, convolve_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro","page":"Public Functions","text":"convolve_rt_macro(xs, ys, ζ_rt, μ)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6), assuming equal radial and tangential velocity scales (ζ_R = ζ_T).\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum (or ys if ζ_rt == 0).\n\nSee also: rt_macro_kernel, convolve_iso_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.gray_rot_kernel","page":"Public Functions","text":"gray_rot_kernel(vs, vsini, u1)\n\nCompute the Gray (2008) rotation broadening kernel (Eq. 18.14) with linear limb darkening.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid centered on the line core (m/s).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized rotation kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_gray_rotation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.gray_rot_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel","page":"Public Functions","text":"hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel(σs, vsini, ζ_rt; u1=0.43, u2=0.31, intres=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS)\n\nCompute the Fourier transform of the Hirano et al. (2011) rotation+macroturbulence kernel (Eq. B12) by quadrature integration over the visible stellar disk.\n\nArguments:\n\nσs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Frequency grid (s/m; inverse of velocity units).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nu1::Real=0.43: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nu2::Real=0.31: Quadratic limb-darkening coefficient.\nintres::Int=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS: Number of quadrature points for the disk integral.\n\nReturns:\n\nKσ::Vector{<:Real}: Fourier transform of the combined kernel, same length as σs.\n\nSee also: convolve_hirano_rotmacro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel","page":"Public Functions","text":"gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_rt)\n\nCompute the isotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.8), assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT) and equal velocity scales (ζR = ζT). The kernel is the disk-integrated (μ-averaged) form.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid centered on the line core (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Isotropic macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_iso_rt_macro, rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel","page":"Public Functions","text":"rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_rt, μ)\n\nCompute the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6), assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT) and equal velocity scales (ζR = ζT). The radial and the tangential Gaussian are each normalized over the samples of vs, so both carry the weight 1/2 even where one is narrower than a pixel (μ → 1, μ → 0).\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid (m/s), uniform, with a sample at zero velocity.\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro, gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\nrt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_r, ζ_t, μ)\n\nCompute the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6) with independent radial and tangential velocity scales, assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT). The radial and the tangential Gaussian are each normalized over the samples of vs, so both carry the weight 1/2 even where one is narrower than a pixel.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid (m/s), uniform, with a sample at zero velocity.\nζ_r::Real: Radial macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nζ_t::Real: Tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.ceiling_ratio","page":"Public Functions","text":"ceiling_ratio(cfunc_dt, τ_ref) -> Vector\nceiling_ratio(result::FormTempResult) -> Vector\n\nPer-wavelength top-of-atmosphere contamination statistic: the topmost interval's flux contribution density as a fraction of the column peak density.\n\nNear 0 when the contribution function decays inside the model atmosphere, near 1 when it is still rising at the top layer, where the formation temperature is a lower limit set by where the model was truncated. Threshold with boundary_mask; also stored on FormTempResult.\n\nτ_ref is required because this compares two intervals: on the native MARCS grid the top interval is twice as wide as one in the finely-sampled region, which inflates a bare ratio of integrals by ~2× and over-flags. cfunc_per_dex divides that measure out.\n\nAn all-zero column yields 0, not NaN. form_temps_from_cfunc reports those wavelengths instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.ceiling_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.boundary_mask","page":"Public Functions","text":"boundary_mask(cfunc_dt, τ_ref; r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH) -> BitVector\nboundary_mask(result::FormTempResult; r_thresh=result.r_thresh) -> BitVector\n\nFlag wavelengths whose formation temperature is contaminated by the top of the model atmosphere, i.e. where ceiling_ratio exceeds r_thresh.\n\nFor a FormTempResult the threshold defaults to the one the calculation used, so the mask matches the wavelengths it warned about. Pass r_thresh to re-threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.boundary_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.cfunc_per_dex","page":"Public Functions","text":"cfunc_per_dex(cfunc_dt, τ_ref) -> Matrix\ncfunc_per_dex(result::FormTempResult) -> Matrix\n\nConvert a contribution function from per-interval integrals to a density per dex of reference optical depth, dF / dlog₁₀τ_ref.\n\ncont_func holds integrals: sum(cont_func, dims=1) is the emergent flux, so the layer measure is baked into each element. Sums over it are therefore independent of the layer grid, but plotting it against depth, or comparing one interval against another, is not — the native MARCS grid changes spacing by 2× at log τ_ref = -3 and +1, which shows up as a step of the same size. Divide by the interval width first.\n\nUse this for plotting and for any across-layer comparison. Do not use it for weighted sums over depth: those need the integrals, where the measure cancels (see form_temps_from_cfunc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.cfunc_per_dex"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.form_temps_from_cfunc","page":"Public Functions","text":"form_temps_from_cfunc(cfunc_dt, Ts; τ_ref=T[], r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH,\n                      warn_boundary=true) -> Vector\n\nFormation temperature at 50% of the cumulative flux contribution.\n\nThe cumulative is node-anchored: F = 0 at the top node Ts[1], and the contribution accumulated through interval k is assigned to the deep node Ts[k+1], so the 50% crossing interpolates against node temperatures Ts. Interval-center temperatures (elav(Ts)) would bias the result half an interval cool.\n\ncfunc_dt is (Natm-1, Nλ) (per-interval flux contribution); Ts is (Natm,) node temperatures. CPU; GPU callers pass host copies.\n\ncfunc_dt must be the per-interval integrals, not a density: the cumulative sum is what makes this independent of the layer grid, because each element already carries its own interval width. Passing cfunc_per_dex output here would drop that weighting.\n\nTwo degenerate cases warn:\n\nNon-positive column total: no median exists, so the formation temperature is NaN. Reachable when an upstream microturbulence kernel underflows and zeros a column.\nBoundary contamination: where ceiling_ratio exceeds r_thresh the contribution is still peaking at the top layer, so the formation temperature is a lower limit near Ts[1]. Flags the same wavelengths as boundary_mask at that r_thresh; warn_boundary=false silences it.\n\nτ_ref is used only by that second warning, which compares intervals and so needs the layer measure. Omitting it falls back to a uniform measure, matching what ceiling_ratio does when no reference grid exists, so the warned set always equals the masked set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.form_temps_from_cfunc"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.AlphaCache","page":"Public Functions","text":"AlphaCache(wls, A_X, Nlayers; cntm_step=ANGSTROM_TO_CM, line_buffer=10*ANGSTROM_TO_CM)\nAlphaCache(wls, A_X, atm; cntm_step=ANGSTROM_TO_CM, line_buffer=10*ANGSTROM_TO_CM)\n\nReusable cache for accelerated compute_alpha! calls.\n\nThis cache implements:\n\nwarm-started electron density guesses across columns,\nprecomputed abundance normalization and continuum wavelength grid,\nreusable buffers for chemistry and line-absorption assembly.\n\nArguments:\n\nwls::Korg.Wavelengths: Wavelength grid for line and continuum absorption.\nA_X::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Elemental abundances on the usual astronomical scale.\nNlayers::Int or atm::Atmosphere: Number of atmosphere layers, or an atmosphere struct.\ncntm_step: Continuum wavelength step (cm; default ANGSTROM_TO_CM, i.e. 1 Å).\nline_buffer: Buffer around the wavelength range for line wings (cm; default 10 Å).\n\nSee also: compute_alpha!, set_abundances!, reset_alpha_cache!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AlphaCache"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.round_to_power","page":"Public Functions","text":"round_to_power(x::Real)\n\nRound x to one significant digit based on its order of magnitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.round_to_power"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.elav","page":"Public Functions","text":"elav(a::AbstractVector)\nelav(a; dims)\n\nCompute midpoints between adjacent elements along dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.elav"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FormationTemps.searchsortednearest","page":"Public Functions","text":"searchsortednearest(a, x)\nsearchsortednearest(x, a)\n\nReturn the index of the element in sorted vector a that is closest to x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.searchsortednearest"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Large-Linelists-and-Chunked-Synthesis","page":"Large Linelists","text":"A linelist covering hundreds or thousands of Angstroms may not fit in GPU memory in a single call, and may simply be too slow to compute all at once. calc_formation_temp_chunked splits the wavelength range into fixed-width chunks and computes each one independently. It hands back the raw per-chunk results and leaves the stitching to you.","title":"Large Linelists & Chunked Synthesis"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#In-memory-workflow","page":"Large Linelists","text":"By default we get back a Vector{FormTempResult}, one element per chunk:\n\nusing Korg\nusing FormationTemps; FT = FormationTemps\n\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))\nstar = StellarProps(Teff=5777.0, logg=4.44, Fe_H=0.0,\n                    vsini=2100.0, v_macro=3400.0, v_micro=850.0)\n\nchunks = calc_formation_temp_chunked(star, linelist;\n    chunk_width=50.0, wing_padding=30.0, overlap=5.0,\n    Δλ=0.01, method=:quadrature)\n\nEach element is a full FormTempResult, so we have wavs, flux, form_temps, cont_func, ceiling_ratio, r_thresh, and atmosphere for every chunk. Adjacent chunks overlap by overlap Angstroms, which gives us room to blend across the seam or to cut at its midpoint.\n\nOne thing to watch out for: ceiling_ratio is a per-chunk reduction, so we should not just concatenate the per-chunk vectors when stitching. Recompute it from the stitched contribution function instead. ceiling_ratio will accept a bare matrix, along with the reference optical depth grid it needs in order to compare layer intervals of unequal width:\n\nr = ceiling_ratio(stitched_cont_func, get_τs(chunks[1].atmosphere))","title":"In-memory workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Choosing-a-method","page":"Large Linelists","text":"Chunking multiplies the cost of a single calc_formation_temp call by the number of chunks, so the integration method matters a great deal here. method=:quadrature reproduces the :disk physics, inclination and differential rotation included, at a fraction of the cost. Reach for it before coarsening Nϕ.","title":"Choosing a method"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Streaming-to-disk","page":"Large Linelists","text":"For a long spectrum the accumulated results will not fit in memory. Passing a callback writes each chunk out as it completes, and calc_formation_temp_chunked then returns nothing rather than accumulating anything. Here is the callback from scripts/generate_temp_spectrum.jl, which writes each chunk into its own HDF5 group:\n\n        # callback writes each chunk to HDF5 as it completes\n        function write_chunk(ci, result, ll_chunk)\n            chunk_idx[] = ci\n            # relabel once, then store and mask against the same axis\n            wavs = to_output_frame(collect(result.wavs))\n            line_centers = to_output_frame([l.wl * 1e8 for l in ll_chunk])\n            g = create_group(h5, @sprintf(\"chunk_%04d\", ci))\n            g[\"line_centers\"] = line_centers\n            g[\"wavs\"] = wavs\n            g[\"flux\"] = result.flux\n            g[\"temp\"] = result.form_temps\n            g[\"cfunc\"] = result.cont_func\n            r = FT.ceiling_ratio(result)\n            g[\"ceiling_ratio\"] = r\n            # growth truncates at chunk edges — Ha's extent exceeds chunk_width — so the\n            # blended 1D output below is the authoritative mask\n            write_mask!(g, wavs, result.flux, result.cont_func)\n        end\n\n\n\nThe callback signature is (chunk_idx::Int, result::FormTempResult, ll_chunk) -> nothing, where ll_chunk is the padded linelist view used for that chunk.","title":"Streaming to disk"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Chunking-parameters","page":"Large Linelists","text":"# chunked computation parameters\nchunk_width = 50.0    # Å per wavelength chunk\nwing_padding = 30.0   # Å beyond chunk edges for linelist selection\noverlap = 5.0         # Å overlap between chunks for stitching\nΔλ = 0.001\nNϕ = 128\nbuffer = 3.0\n\n# disk-integration method: true → :quadrature (fast ring-by-ring μ-quadrature),\n# false → :disk (explicit tiling reference). Nμ/N_az are the quadrature node counts.\nuse_quadrature = true\nNμ = 32\nN_az = 256\nintegration_method = use_quadrature ? :quadrature : :disk\nquad_kwargs = use_quadrature ? (Nμ=Nμ, N_az=N_az) : NamedTuple()\n\n\n\nParameter Default Description\nchunk_width 50.0 Width of each wavelength chunk in Angstroms.\nwing_padding 30.0 Extra range (Angstroms) beyond each chunk edge for linelist selection, so that broad wings (H-alpha, Ca II) still contribute at chunk boundaries.\noverlap 5.0 Overlap width (Angstroms) between adjacent chunks, for blending or cutting when stitching.\nΔλ 0.001 Wavelength step size in Angstroms.\nbuffer 2.0 Extra wavelength range (Angstroms) beyond the linelist edges.\n\nmethod, Nϕ, Nμ, N_az, use_gpu, gpu_precision, r_thresh and ne_warn_thresh are forwarded unchanged to calc_formation_temp.\n\ntip: Choosing `wing_padding`\nIf this is too small, lines near a chunk edge lose their wings and we are left with residuals at the stitch boundaries. 30 Angstroms is conservative; 15 is enough for all but the broadest lines.\n\ntip: Choosing `overlap`\nThe broadening kernels pad by edge replication, so within one kernel half-support of a chunk edge the result draws on replicated samples rather than on the true neighboring spectrum. Cutting at the overlap midpoint only discards that band if overlap is larger than twice the half-support, roughly (v sin i + 3zeta + 3xi)c cdot lambda. For a solar-like star this sits comfortably inside the 5-Angstrom default, but it scales with v sin i, and by 100 km/s the default is marginal. Scale overlap up for rapid rotators.","title":"Chunking parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Stitching-chunks","page":"Large Linelists","text":"Here we read the chunks back and crossfade the flux, formation temperatures, and contribution functions across each overlap. The accumulator is preallocated at its final width, since growing it one chunk at a time costs O(N^2) bytes copied:\n\nfunction blend_chunks(h5in, chunk_names; overlap, Δλ)\n    nchunks = length(chunk_names)\n\n    # the width arithmetic requires every chunk to share one Nλ; calc_formation_temp_chunked\n    # rounds its upper bound up so that holds, but check before allocating rather than assume\n    Nλ_chunk = length(h5in[first(chunk_names)][\"wavs\"])\n    for gn in chunk_names\n        @assert length(h5in[gn][\"wavs\"]) == Nλ_chunk \"chunk $gn has non-uniform Nλ\"\n    end\n\n    N_ov = max(0, round(Int, overlap / Δλ) + 1)\n    n_tail = Nλ_chunk - N_ov\n    total = Nλ_chunk + (nchunks - 1) * n_tail\n    @assert n_tail > 0 \"overlap must be smaller than chunk_width\"\n\n    c1 = read_chunk(h5in, first(chunk_names))\n    Nrows = size(c1.cfunc, 1)\n\n    all_wavs = Vector{Float64}(undef, total)\n    all_flux = Vector{Float64}(undef, total)\n    all_temps = Vector{Float64}(undef, total)\n    all_cfunc = Matrix{Float64}(undef, Nrows, total)\n    all_lc = Float64[]\n\n    all_wavs[1:Nλ_chunk] .= c1.wavs\n    all_flux[1:Nλ_chunk] .= c1.flux\n    all_temps[1:Nλ_chunk] .= c1.temp\n    all_cfunc[:, 1:Nλ_chunk] .= c1.cfunc\n    append!(all_lc, c1.lc)\n    off = Nλ_chunk\n\n    for i in 2:nchunks\n        c = read_chunk(h5in, chunk_names[i])\n\n        # crossfade the overlap: accumulator cols (off-N_ov+1):off against chunk cols 1:N_ov\n        @inbounds for k in 1:N_ov\n            w_new = Float64(k) / Float64(N_ov + 1)\n            w_old = 1.0 - w_new\n            d = off - N_ov + k\n            all_flux[d] = w_old * all_flux[d] + w_new * c.flux[k]\n            all_temps[d] = w_old * all_temps[d] + w_new * c.temp[k]\n            @views all_cfunc[:, d] .= w_old .* all_cfunc[:, d] .+ w_new .* c.cfunc[:, k]\n        end\n\n        # write the non-overlapping tail in place\n        dst = (off + 1):(off + n_tail)\n        src = (N_ov + 1):Nλ_chunk\n        @views all_wavs[dst] .= c.wavs[src]\n        @views all_flux[dst] .= c.flux[src]\n        @views all_temps[dst] .= c.temp[src]\n        @views all_cfunc[:, dst] .= c.cfunc[:, src]\n        # no need to filter to the new tail: line centres are bit-identical across\n        # chunks (same Line objects), so sort(unique(·)) and the trim below dedupe\n        append!(all_lc, c.lc)\n        off += n_tail\n    end\n    @assert off == total\n    return (wavs = all_wavs, flux = all_flux, temps = all_temps, cfunc = all_cfunc,\n            lc = sort(unique(all_lc)))\nend\n\n","title":"Stitching chunks"},{"category":"section","location":"chunked/#Complete-example","page":"Large Linelists","text":"scripts/generate_temp_spectrum.jl puts all of the above together. It streams chunked results to HDF5, reads the raw chunks back and stitches them, trims to the linelist extent, and writes the spliced spectrum alongside the model atmosphere and a per-pixel quality mask.\n\nnote: Air and vacuum wavelengths\nThe synthesis runs on the vacuum grid that read_linelist returns, and the script applies an air axis only as a relabeling of the stored output. When stitching, relabel after blending — the crossfade indexing assumes a uniform grid, and an air axis is very slightly non-uniform. See Air and vacuum wavelengths in the Basic Tutorial for why the linelist itself must not be rewritten.","title":"Complete example"},{"category":"section","location":"longlist/#Full-Index","page":"Full Index","text":"","title":"Full Index"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.AbstractConvolutionMemory","page":"Full Index","text":"AbstractConvolutionMemory{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nAbstract supertype for GPU convolution memory structs.  Concrete subtypes:\n\nConvolutionMemory: micro-turbulence path (Doppler filter + batched FFT).\nMacroConvolutionMemory: macro-turbulence / rotation path (adds 1D kernel FFT plans, padded kernel buffers, and an output extraction buffer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AbstractConvolutionMemory"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.AtmosphereCPU","page":"Full Index","text":"AtmosphereCPU{T<:AbstractFloat} <: Atmosphere{T}\n\nCPU-backed atmosphere wrapping a Korg MARCS model.\n\nFields:\n\nNatm: Number of atmosphere layers.\nτs: Reference optical depth grid (length Natm); empty if tau_ref is unavailable.\nzs, Ts: Height (cm) and temperature (K) grids.\nnₑ, nd: Electron and total number density grids.\nreference_wavelength: MARCS reference wavelength for τ_ref (cm; typically 5000 Å).\nvx, vy, vz: Per-layer velocity components (m/s).\nv_mic, v_los: Per-layer microturbulent speed and mean line-of-sight velocity (m/s).\n\nSee also: AtmosphereCPU(atm_korg)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AtmosphereCPU"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.AtmosphereCPU-Tuple{Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"AtmosphereCPU(atm_korg)\n\nConstruct an AtmosphereCPU with thermodynamic and velocity fields on the CPU.\n\nThe atmosphere's native (non-uniform log-τ) layer grid is used as-is; the τ integrators consume the actual per-interval spacing, as Korg's do. If the model does not supply tau_ref (e.g. some non-MARCS grids), atm.τs is set to an empty vector and the Bézier τ integrator is used downstream instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AtmosphereCPU"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU","page":"Full Index","text":"AtmosphereGPU{T<:AbstractFloat} <: Atmosphere{T}\n\nGPU-backed atmosphere wrapping a Korg MARCS model.\n\nFields:\n\nNatm: Number of atmosphere layers.\nτs: Reference optical depth grid (length Natm); empty if tau_ref is unavailable.\nzs, Ts: Height (cm) and temperature (K) grids on the CPU.\nnₑ, nd: Electron and total number density grids on the CPU.\nreference_wavelength: MARCS reference wavelength for τ_ref (cm; typically 5000 Å).\nzs_gpu, Ts_gpu, nd_gpu: Height, temperature, and number density on the device.\nvx, vy, vz: Per-layer velocity components on the device (m/s). Initialized to zero by the convenience constructor; populated by downstream packages from MHD simulation data.\nv_mic, v_los: Per-layer microturbulent speed and mean line-of-sight velocity on the device (m/s).\n\nSee also: AtmosphereGPU(atm_korg)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU-Tuple{Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"AtmosphereGPU(atm_korg; T=Float64)\n\nConstruct an AtmosphereGPU{T} with thermodynamic fields from Korg and velocity fields allocated on the GPU. Pass T=Float32 for single-precision GPU arrays.\n\nKorg always returns Float64 data; the constructor converts all fields (CPU and GPU) to type T.\n\nThe atmosphere's native (non-uniform log-τ) layer grid is used as-is; the τ integrators consume the actual per-interval spacing, as Korg's do. If the model does not supply tau_ref (e.g. some non-MARCS grids), atm.τs is set to an empty vector and the Bézier τ integrator is used downstream instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.BatchedMicroConvMem","page":"Full Index","text":"BatchedMicroConvMem{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nPre-allocated GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for batched Doppler convolution of B tiles simultaneously. The signal FFT is shared across tiles (absorption is tile-independent); only the Doppler filter and convolution product scale with B.\n\nLayout: first dimension is B*Natm for per-tile buffers, Natm for shared buffers. Tile b occupies rows (b-1)*Natm+1 : b*Natm.\n\nSee also: ConvolutionMemory, BatchedMicroConvMem(Nλ, Natm, B, Npad)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.BatchedMicroConvMem"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.BatchedMicroConvMem-NTuple{4, Int64}","page":"Full Index","text":"BatchedMicroConvMem(Nλ, Natm, B, Npad; T=Float64)\n\nAllocate GPU buffers for batched Doppler convolution of B tiles, each with Natm atmosphere rows and Nλ wavelength points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.BatchedMicroConvMem"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.CPUTileWorkspace","page":"Full Index","text":"Pre-allocated per-thread working arrays and FFT infrastructure for the CPU disk integration tile loop. Eliminates per-tile heap allocations so the threaded loop scales without GC contention. Uses padded linear convolution with edge replication and R2C FFT plans, matching the GPU path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.CPUTileWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.ConvolutionMemory","page":"Full Index","text":"ConvolutionMemory{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nPre-allocated GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for batched FFT-based Doppler convolution (micro-turbulence path).\n\nSee also: MacroConvolutionMemory, ConvolutionMemory(Nλ, Natm, Npad)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.ConvolutionMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.ConvolutionMemory-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Full Index","text":"ConvolutionMemory(Nλ, Natm, Npad; T=Float64)\n\nAllocate GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for micro-turbulence convolution of Natm × Nλ matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.ConvolutionMemory"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.GPUMemory","page":"Full Index","text":"GPUMemory{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nPre-allocated GPU working arrays for the radiative transfer computation.\n\nFields:\n\nλs: Wavelength grid on the device (Å), length Nλ.\nαs, τs: Absorption coefficient and optical depth arrays, shape (Natm, Nλ).\ncfunc, cfunc_dt: Contribution function and dτ-weighted variant, shape (Natm-1, Nλ).\ntau_ds, tau_alphaC: Bézier τ-integration geometry work arrays; used when use_anchored=false.\nlog_τ_ref, ifactor_base: Anchored τ-integration constants (log τ_ref and τ_ref / α_ref); populated and used when use_anchored=true.\nuse_anchored: true for the anchored d(log τ) integrator; false for the Bézier integrator.\nv_los_zeros: Pre-allocated zero vector (length Natm) for stationary-frame flux calculations. Used instead of destructively zeroing atm.v_los.\n\nSee also: GPUMemory(λs, atm), GPUMemory(λs, atm, α_ref)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.GPUMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.GPUMemory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"GPUMemory(λs_cpu, atm, α_ref_cpu)\n\nAllocate GPU working memory using the anchored τ integrator.  α_ref_cpu is the continuum absorption coefficient at the MARCS reference wavelength for each layer — αs_cont[:, end] from a prior compute_alpha! call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.GPUMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.GPUMemory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"GPUMemory(λs_cpu, atm)\n\nAllocate GPU working memory using the Bezier τ integrator (backward-compatible default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.GPUMemory"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory","page":"Full Index","text":"MacroConvolutionMemory{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nPre-allocated GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for macro-turbulence / rotation convolution. Extends the base convolution buffers with 1D kernel FFT infrastructure, padded kernel work vectors, and an output extraction buffer.\n\nSee also: ConvolutionMemory, MacroConvolutionMemory(Nλ, Natm, Npad)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Full Index","text":"MacroConvolutionMemory(Nλ, Natm, Npad; T=Float64)\n\nAllocate GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for macro-turbulence / rotation convolution of Natm × Nλ matrices. Includes 1D kernel FFT infrastructure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory"},{"category":"type","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.StackedRenderMemory","page":"Full Index","text":"StackedRenderMemory{T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nPre-allocated GPU buffers and cuFFT plans for rendering the emergent intensity of B atmospheres (\"components\") in a single set of kernel launches. Every component has its own wavelength grid; the grids share one length Nλ and one padded length L.\n\nLayout: the first dimension of every stacked buffer is B*Natm; component b occupies rows (b-1)*Natm+1 : b*Natm (component_rows). The stacked wavelength grids are vectors of length B*Nλ; the grid of component b occupies elements (b-1)*Nλ+1 : b*Nλ.\n\nFields the caller touches:\n\nv_los, v_mic: per-layer line-of-sight velocity and microturbulent speed (m/s), length B*Natm; filled by the caller before each render_stacked!.\nintensity: emergent intensity, shape (Nλ, B); filled by render_stacked!. It is the caller's matrix when one was passed to the constructor.\nrows_independent: whether a stacked component is bit-identical to its serial render. cuFFT transforms the rows of an odd-length real plan in consecutive pairs, so this holds only for an even Natm or an even padded length. render_stacked! asserts it.\n\nEvery array field is set once by the constructor and never reassigned, so views of v_los, v_mic and intensity stay valid for the life of the struct.\n\nSee also: StackedRenderMemory(mems, cmems, atms, μ), set_signal_ft!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.StackedRenderMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.StackedRenderMemory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{<:FormationTemps.GPUMemory{T}}, AbstractVector{<:FormationTemps.ConvolutionMemory{T}}, AbstractVector{<:FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU{T}}, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"StackedRenderMemory(mems, cmems, atms, μ; intensity=nothing)\nStackedRenderMemory(mem, cmem, atms, μ; intensity=nothing)\n\nAllocate the buffers for a stacked render of the atmospheres atms at disk position μ. Component b is rendered on the wavelength grid of mems[b] and cmems[b]: the GPUMemory and the ConvolutionMemory its serial render uses. The three vectors have one entry per component, and several components may share a memory. Every cmem must have the same Nλ, Natm, L and pad_left, every mem a grid of Nλ wavelengths and every atmosphere Natm layers. The second form puts every component on the one grid of mem and cmem.\n\nintensity is the matrix render_stacked! writes to: nothing allocates one; a CuArray{T,2} of size (Nλ, B) is used as it is, and may be a contiguous column range of a larger matrix taken with view.\n\nThe wavelength grid of every mem and the temperatures and Bézier geometry of every atmosphere are copied here, once; later changes to mem.λs, zs_gpu or Ts_gpu are not seen. Nothing is rendered, and a cmem need not have been used yet: its Doppler-kernel grid, kernel offsets and σ_floor are read on the first render.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.StackedRenderMemory"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.StellarProps-Tuple{}","page":"Full Index","text":"StellarProps(; Teff=NaN, logg=NaN, Fe_H=NaN, vsini=0.0, v_macro=NaN, v_micro=NaN,\n              ρstar=1.0, istar=90.0, α₂=0.0, α₄=0.0)\n\nContainer for stellar parameters and broadening properties used by calc_formation_temp.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nTeff: effective temperature (K).\nlogg: log10 surface gravity (cgs).\nFe_H: metallicity [Fe/H] (dex); used to build the abundance vector A_X.\nvsini: projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nv_macro: macroturbulent velocity scale ζ (m/s); if NaN, uses vmac_fit(Teff, logg).\nv_micro: microturbulent velocity ξ (m/s). Scalar for uniform broadening; vector of length Natm for per-layer broadening. If scalar NaN, uses vmic_fit(Teff).\nρstar: stellar radius scale factor for disk integration (dimensionless; default 1). No effect on any output: cont_func is a flux per unit surface area, and the rotation field depends only on x_sky/ρstar.\nistar: stellar inclination (degrees; 90 = equator-on). No physical effect for rigid rotation (α₂=α₄=0), where v_los = -vsini·x_sky/ρstar and the broadening follows from the projected vsini alone. It matters once differential rotation is on, where it selects which latitude bands are visible and how they are weighted. Numerically it is an exact no-op for method=:quadrature; for method=:disk at finite Nϕ it changes which tiles are visible, shifting the result at the discretization level.\nα₂, α₄: differential-rotation coefficients in the normalized rate law Ω(ϕ)/Ω_eq = f(ϕ) = 1 - α₂·sin²ϕ - α₄·sin⁴ϕ. Default 0 (solid body); positive values make the equator rotate faster than the poles. vsini stays the projected equatorial velocity (f(0)=1).\n\nStruct fields:\n\nTeff, logg, Fe_H, A_X: atmosphere parameters (A_X is the full abundance vector).\nvsini, ζ, ξ: rotational, macroturbulent, and microturbulent velocities (m/s). ξ is T (scalar) or AbstractVector{T} (per-layer).\nρstar, istar, α₂, α₄: disk integration parameters (α₂, α₄ are the differential-rotation coefficients).\n\nSee also: vmac_fit, vmic_fit, calc_formation_temp\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.StellarProps"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._add_hydrogen_line_absorption!-Tuple{Any, Korg.Wavelengths, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Float64}","page":"Full Index","text":"_add_hydrogen_line_absorption!(αs, wls, Ts, nₑs, nH_I, nHe_I, partition_funcs,\n                               window_size_Å; use_MHD=nothing)\n\nAdd hydrogen (Balmer/Brackett) line opacity to αs in-place, one atmosphere layer per row, mirroring Korg.synthesize. Korg computes hydrogen lines from dedicated Stark/MHD physics rather than from the linelist, so Korg.line_absorption! never emits them.\n\nNotes:\n\nξ = 0: microturbulence is applied downstream via FFT convolution on αs (as for the metal lines, which are passed vmic=0); passing a nonzero ξ here would double-count it.\nNot added to the 5000 Å reference opacity α_ref, matching Korg, which builds α5 from the continuum plus a 5000 Å linelist and adds hydrogen lines only to α.\nuse_MHD=nothing (the default) enables the MHD occupation-probability formalism only for wls[end] < 13000 Å. Korg's use_MHD_for_hydrogen_lines defaults to true at all wavelengths and warns above 13000 Å that false is preferable; this default applies that recommendation directly. Pass use_MHD=true to match Korg when comparing in the near-IR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._add_hydrogen_line_absorption!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._allequal-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Full Index","text":"_allequal(v)\n\nCheck whether all elements of v are equal (short-circuits on first mismatch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._allequal"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._apply_fft_kernel!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, Array{Complex{T}, 1}, FormationTemps.CPUTileWorkspace, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_apply_fft_kernel!(out, ys, kernel_ft, ws, Nrows)\n\nConvolve the first Nrows rows of ys with a pre-computed kernel FFT, writing results into out. Uses padded linear convolution with edge replication and R2C FFT plans from ws (zero heap allocation per row).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._apply_fft_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._calc_formation_temp_quadrature_cpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StellarProps, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_calc_formation_temp_quadrature_cpu(star, linelist; Δλ, minλ, maxλ, buffer,\n                                    Nμ=32, N_az=256, kwargs...)\n\nRing-by-ring μ-quadrature evaluation of the disk-integrated flux formation temperatures (CPU). Produces the same FormTempResult as the explicit tiling path (method=:disk). Nμ sets the number of Gauss–Legendre μ nodes; N_az the azimuthal sampling of the per-ring Doppler kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._calc_formation_temp_quadrature_cpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._calc_formation_temp_quadrature_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StellarProps, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_calc_formation_temp_quadrature_gpu(star, linelist; Δλ, minλ, maxλ, buffer,\n                                    gpu_precision=Float64, Nμ=32, N_az=256, kwargs...)\n\nGPU port of the ring-by-ring μ-quadrature disk integration. Loops the Nμ Gauss–Legendre μ nodes using the single-tile GPU intensity path and the GPU RT-macro convolution, applying rotation as a per-ring Doppler convolution. Produces the same FormTempResult as _calc_formation_temp_quadrature_cpu.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._calc_formation_temp_quadrature_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._cfunc_measure-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_cfunc_measure(T, τ_ref, Natm1) -> Vector{T}\n\nPer-interval width of the reference optical-depth grid, in dex. The measure any statistic that compares one interval of a contribution function against another must divide out.\n\nAn empty τ_ref (the Bézier τ path, which has no reference grid) falls back to a uniform measure, reproducing a bare reduction over the per-interval integrals. That result is grid-dependent, so it warns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._cfunc_measure"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._convolve_macro_inplace!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T, FormationTemps.CPUTileWorkspace}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_convolve_macro_inplace!(out, xs, ys, ζ_rt, μ, ws)\n\nIn-place radial-tangential macroturbulence convolution using pre-allocated CPUTileWorkspace buffers. Uses padded linear convolution with edge replication and R2C FFTs, matching the GPU path. When ζ_rt == 0, copies ys into out directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._convolve_macro_inplace!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._convolve_micro_inplace!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T, FormationTemps.CPUTileWorkspace}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_convolve_micro_inplace!(out, xs, ys, v_los, v_mic, ws)\n\nIn-place microturbulent broadening using pre-allocated CPUTileWorkspace buffers.\n\nScalar v_los and v_mic build one kernel and apply it to all atmosphere layers (the common case in disk integration). Vector arguments build per-row kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._convolve_micro_inplace!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._extract_korg_fields-Tuple{Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"_extract_korg_fields(atm_korg)\n\nExtract thermodynamic fields from a Korg model atmosphere into a NamedTuple, on the atmosphere's native layer grid. Shared by both AtmosphereCPU and AtmosphereGPU constructors.\n\nThe native (non-uniform log-τ) MARCS grid is used as-is; the τ integrators consume the actual per-interval spacing, as Korg's do. Call _resample_log_tau on atm_korg beforehand to resample onto a uniform log-τ grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._extract_korg_fields"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._finalize_kernel_ft!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory{T}, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_finalize_kernel_ft!(cmem, i0)\n\nTake the normalized real-space kernel in cmem.padded_kernel_gpu, whose zero-lag sample sits at padded index pad_left + i0, roll it to the padded center, ifftshift to DFT ordering, R2C-FFT via plan_fwd_1d, and return a copy of the kernel FT (CuVector{Complex}) for convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached. Uses cmem.shift_kernel_gpu / cmem.kr_1d / cmem.kernel_row_ft_1d as scratch. Shared by precompute_rt_macro_kernel_ft and the quadrature ring-kernel builder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._finalize_kernel_ft!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._ifftshift_complex!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{Complex{T}, 1}, Vector{T}}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"_ifftshift_complex!(dst, src)\n\nCopy real vector src into complex vector dst with ifftshift permutation (zero allocation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._ifftshift_complex!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._kernel_to_dft_layout!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Vector{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"_kernel_to_dft_layout!(kbuf, kernel, i0)\n\nPlace a length-Nλ kernel (with zero-lag at index i0) into a length-L DFT-ordered buffer kbuf (zero-lag at index 1, positive lags next, negative lags at end).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._kernel_to_dft_layout!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._pad_edges!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any, Int64, Int64}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"_pad_edges!(dst, src, pad_left, Nλ)\n\nFill length-L vector dst with edge-replicated padding of src: left pad = src[1], right pad = src[Nλ].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._pad_edges!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._padded_convolve-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Vector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_padded_convolve(ys::Vector, kernel::Vector; Npad=512)\n_padded_convolve(ys::Matrix, kernel::Vector; Npad=512)\n\nCPU padded linear convolution with edge replication using R2C FFT. Matches the GPU convolution strategy (padded signal, zero-padded kernel, extract valid region). Returns an array the same size as ys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._padded_convolve"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._resample_log_tau-Tuple{Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"_resample_log_tau(atm_korg; n_layers=-1)\n\nResample a Korg model atmosphere onto a uniform grid in log(τref), returning a new atmosphere of the same type. Returns `atmkorg` unchanged if tau_ref is unavailable.\n\nNot called by the atmosphere constructors, which use the native layer grid. Available for callers that want uniform log-τ spacing: interpolate_marcs drops layers where the interpolated grid has NaN values (via nanmask), leaving a non-uniform spacing.\n\nBy default n_layers matches the input layer count, making this a spacing fix only. Pass an explicit value to upsample or downsample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._resample_log_tau"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._ring_doppler_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, T, T, T, T, AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_ring_doppler_kernel(μ_k, vsini, iₛ, α₂, α₄, λs, N_az)\n\nBuild the normalized line-of-sight-velocity distribution for a ring at projected radius r_k = √(1-μ_k²), as a length-Nλ kernel centered at zero velocity (index i0 = Nλ÷2+1) on the wavelength grid λs. Convolving a ring's spectrum with this kernel performs the azimuthal average of Doppler-shifted spectra.\n\nλs supplies only the length and the velocity step, and need not be of the kernel's precision: pass the grid at the precision it was generated at. The kernel has the precision of μ_k.\n\nThe kernel is the area-exact bin-integrated LOS velocity distribution, computed two ways depending on the rotation law:\n\nα₂ = α₄ = 0 (solid body, the default): analytically via the arcsine CDF, _ring_kernel_rigid!. Inclination-independent.\notherwise: by arc-overlap integration over an even number of azimuthal arcs, _ring_kernel_diffrot!. N_az floors the arc count and only matters here.\n\nBoth are symmetric in velocity, so the kernel carries no spurious radial-velocity shift. Weight shifted outside the wavelength window is discarded and the kernel renormalized, which narrows the broadening; a warning fires, since it means the synthesis window is too narrow for vsini.\n\nThe bin weights are exact but their positions are quantized to Δv, which bounds accuracy at low vsini where the kernel spans only a few pixels. See the \"Integration Methods\" guide for tolerances against method=:disk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._ring_doppler_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._ring_kernel_diffrot!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T, T, T, T, T, T, Int64, Int64, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_ring_kernel_diffrot!(K, μ_k, r_k, vsini, iₛ, α₂, α₄, Δv, i0, Nλ, N_az) -> dropped\n\nBin-integrated ring Doppler kernel for differential rotation, written into K. Returns the weight that fell outside the grid.\n\nWith f(ϕ) ≠ 1 the LOS velocity v(az) = -vsini·f(sinϕ(az))·r_k·cos(az) (latitude sinϕ = r_k·sin(az)·cos iₛ + μ_k·sin iₛ) is not a pure cosine and its pushforward has no closed form, so azimuth is sampled. Each arc [az_k, az_{k+1}] carries weight 1/N_fine and maps to the velocity interval spanned by its endpoints; that weight is distributed across the bins that interval overlaps.\n\nEach arc's velocity extent is integrated, not deposited at a point. A linear/CIC deposit, splitting each sample between neighboring bins by linear weights, converges to the true kernel convolved with a spurious ~Δv triangle and over-broadens narrow kernels.\n\nN_fine is forced even. The v ↔ -v symmetry of the exact distribution comes from az → π - az, which flips x_sky while leaving the latitude (hence f) fixed; on the discrete arc partition that map closes only for even N_fine. An odd count leaves K slightly asymmetric, i.e. a spurious radial-velocity shift of order Δv/N_fine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._ring_kernel_diffrot!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._ring_kernel_ft_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory{T}, AbstractVector{T}, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_ring_kernel_ft_gpu(macmem, K, i0)\n\nBuild the Fourier transform of an arbitrary (already normalized) real-space ring Doppler kernel K (length Nλ, centered at i0) in the padded/DFT layout expected by convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached. Places K into the valid region of macmem.padded_kernel_gpu and reuses _finalize_kernel_ft!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._ring_kernel_ft_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._ring_kernel_rigid!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T, Int64, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_ring_kernel_rigid!(K, v_max, Δv, i0, Nλ) -> dropped\n\nBin-integrated ring Doppler kernel for solid-body rotation, written into K. Returns the weight falling outside the grid.\n\nFor α₂ = α₄ = 0 the LOS velocity is v(az) = -v_max·cos(az), v_max = vsini·r_k, independent of inclination. With azimuth uniform on [0, 2π), u = v/v_max follows the arcsine distribution on [-1, 1], with CDF G(u) = asin(u)/π + 1/2. Bin n spans (n - i0 ± 1/2)·Δv, so its weight is the CDF difference across its edges.\n\nasin is odd, so the bin weights come out exactly symmetric and the kernel cannot represent a radial-velocity shift; sampling the distribution instead would lose that. The integrable 1/√(1-u²) singularity at ±v_max needs no special handling because the CDF is finite there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._ring_kernel_rigid!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps._select_lines_for_window-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Vector{Float64}, T, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"_select_lines_for_window(linelist, λ_start, λ_stop, wing_padding)\n\nReturn a view of linelist containing lines whose centers (in Å) fall within [λ_start - wing_padding, λ_stop + wing_padding].  Uses binary search on the sorted linelist for O(log N) per chunk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps._select_lines_for_window"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.batched_macro_multiply_accumulate!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}, CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}, CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}, CUDACore.CuArray{Int32, 1}, CUDACore.CuArray{T, 1}, Int64, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"batched_macro_multiply_accumulate!(acc_ft, signal_ft, kernel_cache_flat,\n                                   μ_idx, dA_tiles, Natm1, Bcur; tile_offset=0)\n\nLaunch the batched macro multiply-accumulate kernel. signal_ft is (Bcur*Natm1, nfreq) from a batched forward FFT of padded cfdt. kernel_cache_flat is (N_unique_μ, nfreq). μ_idx maps tile index → row in kernelcacheflat. Result accumulates into acc_ft (Natm1, nfreq).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.batched_macro_multiply_accumulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.batched_macro_multiply_accumulate_kernel!-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"batched_macro_multiply_accumulate_kernel!(acc_ft, signal_ft, kernel_cache_flat,\n                                          μ_idx, dA_tiles, tile_offset, Natm1, Bcur)\n\nCUDA kernel: for each (layer k, frequency f), loop over B tiles in the batch, multiply the tile's forward-FFT'd signal by its cached macro kernel FFT, weight by dA, and accumulate into the Fourier-space accumulator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.batched_macro_multiply_accumulate_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.boundary_mask-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:AbstractFloat}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}}","page":"Full Index","text":"boundary_mask(cfunc_dt, τ_ref; r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH) -> BitVector\nboundary_mask(result::FormTempResult; r_thresh=result.r_thresh) -> BitVector\n\nFlag wavelengths whose formation temperature is contaminated by the top of the model atmosphere, i.e. where ceiling_ratio exceeds r_thresh.\n\nFor a FormTempResult the threshold defaults to the one the calculation used, so the mask matches the wavelengths it warned about. Pass r_thresh to re-threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.boundary_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_flux_quantities-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU{T}, FormationTemps.GPUMemory, FormationTemps.AbstractConvolutionMemory, Union{CUDACore.CuVector{T}, CUDACore.DenseCuVector{T}, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_flux_quantities(αs_init, atm, mem, cmem, v_mic)\n\nCompute the disk-center (μ=1, zero rotation) flux contribution function and its optical-depth-weighted differential.\n\nApplies microturbulent broadening (zero Doppler shift), integrates the optical depth using either the anchored or Bézier scheme (as configured in mem), and evaluates the Planck-weighted E₂(τ) contribution function at each layer boundary.\n\nArguments:\n\nαs_init::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Absorption coefficients, shape (Natm, Nλ).\natm::AtmosphereGPU: GPU atmosphere.\nmem::GPUMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working arrays.\ncmem::AbstractConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU convolution memory.\nv_mic::Union{T, CuArray{T,1}}: Microturbulent broadening width (m/s). Scalar for uniform broadening; CuVector for per-layer broadening.\n\nReturns:\n\nFluxContFunc with fields:\ncfunc: Flux contribution function C_F, shape (Natm-1, Nλ).\ncfunc_dt: cfunc .* Δτ, the differential contribution.\n\nSee also: calc_intensity_quantities\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_flux_quantities"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StellarProps, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; use_gpu=GPU_DEFAULT, Δλ=0.01,\n                    gpu_precision=Float64, method=:disk,\n                    minλ=NaN, maxλ=NaN, buffer=2.0, u1=NaN, u2=NaN,\n                    Nϕ=128, Nμ=32, N_az=256,\n                    r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH,\n                    showprogress=true, kwargs...)\n\nCompute flux formation temperatures, normalized flux, and flux contribution function for a given star and linelist.\n\nThe wavelength grid is built from the line list (vacuum cm → Å) with padding and step Δλ. Use minλ/maxλ (Angstrom) to override the default bounds (first/last line ± 2 A).\n\nReturns a FormTempResult with fields:\n\nwavs: wavelength grid (Angstrom).\nflux: normalized flux (sum(cfunc_dt_flux) / sum(cfunc_dt_flux_cont)).\nform_temps: formation temperature defined at 50% of the cumulative flux contribution.\ncont_func: contribution function, shape (Natm - 1, Nλ): each interval's contribution to the emergent surface flux F = 2π∫Iμdμ, in the units of Korg.synthesize's flux and on the same scale for every method.\nceiling_ratio: per-wavelength top-of-atmosphere contamination statistic; see ceiling_ratio.\nr_thresh: the contamination threshold used, echoing the r_thresh keyword.\natmosphere: atmosphere structure used for the calculation.\n\nwarning: Formation temperatures can be contaminated by the top of the atmosphere\nWhere the flux contribution is still peaking at the top of the model, form_temps is set by where the model was truncated rather than by the line, and is a lower limit. Expect this in strong saturated cores. result.ceiling_ratio quantifies it per wavelength and boundary_mask flags the affected wavelengths, using the r_thresh recorded on the result so the mask matches what the calculation warned about. r_thresh moves both.\n\nDisk-integration method\n\nmethod selects how the stellar disk is integrated; see the \"Integration Methods\" guide for accuracy and speed trade-offs.\n\n:disk (default) — explicit integration over Nϕ latitude bins. The reference: self-consistent limb darkening, inclination istar, differential rotation (α₂, α₄).\n:quadrature — ring-by-ring Gauss–Legendre μ-quadrature (Nμ nodes, N_az azimuth samples for the per-ring Doppler kernel). Reproduces the :disk physics at a fraction of the cost, solving transfer once per μ node instead of once per tile.\n:hirano — analytic Hirano et al. (2011) rotation + macroturbulence convolution using quadratic limb-darkening coefficients u1, u2. Cheapest, but assumes a parametric limb darkening law and no center-to-limb line-profile variation.\n\nconvolve is a deprecated alias: false ⇒ :disk, true ⇒ :hirano.\n\nAll three methods support use_gpu=true. showprogress=false suppresses the disk integration progress bar.\n\ngpu_precision=Float32 runs GPU computations at single precision, roughly halving GPU memory use. Absorption coefficients are always Float64 (a Korg requirement) and converted before upload. Keep the Float64 default for :quadrature, which loses accuracy at Float32.\n\nThe CPU :disk path is parallelized across tiles with Threads.@threads; launch Julia with multiple threads (julia -t auto) to benefit. See Parallelization.\n\nHydrogen (Balmer/Brackett) lines are included by default, since Korg computes them from dedicated Stark/MHD physics rather than from the linelist. hydrogen_lines=false, hydrogen_line_window_size_Å and use_MHD are forwarded to compute_alpha!.\n\nExamples\n\nstar = StellarProps(Teff=5777.0, logg=4.44, Fe_H=0.0, vsini=2100.0)\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[1:500]\n\n# explicit disk integration (default)\nresult = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01)\n\n# ring-by-ring μ-quadrature\nresult_q = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:quadrature)\n\n# analytic Hirano convolution (needs limb-darkening coefficients)\nresult_h = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:hirano, u1=0.43, u2=0.31)\n\n# Float32 GPU:\nresult32 = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; Δλ=0.01, gpu_precision=Float32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp_chunked-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StellarProps, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_formation_temp_chunked(star, linelist; chunk_width=50.0, wing_padding=30.0,\n                             overlap=5.0, Δλ=0.001, buffer=2.0,\n                             callback=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nCompute formation temperatures over an arbitrarily large wavelength range by processing fixed-width wavelength chunks.\n\nEach chunk selects lines within [λ_start - wing_padding, λ_stop + wing_padding], ensuring that broad line wings contribute correctly at chunk boundaries.\n\nArguments\n\nchunk_width: width of each wavelength chunk in Å.\nwing_padding: extra range (Å) beyond each chunk edge for linelist selection. 30 Å is conservative enough for the strongest lines (H-alpha, Ca II).\noverlap: overlap width (Å) between adjacent chunks for smooth stitching.\ncallback: optional (chunk_idx, result::FormTempResult, ll_chunk) -> nothing called after each chunk, where ll_chunk is the padded linelist view used for that chunk.  When provided, results are not accumulated and the function returns nothing.  Use this for streaming to disk when the full result is too large for RAM.\nAll other keyword arguments are forwarded to calc_formation_temp.\n\nWhen callback=nothing, returns a Vector{FormTempResult} with one entry per chunk.  Stitching is the caller's responsibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_formation_temp_chunked"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_intensity_quantities-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU{T}, FormationTemps.GPUMemory, FormationTemps.AbstractConvolutionMemory, T, CUDACore.CuArray{T, 1}, Union{CUDACore.CuVector{T}, CUDACore.DenseCuVector{T}, T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_intensity_quantities(αs_init, atm, mem, cmem, μ_tile, v_los, v_mic)\n\nCompute the specific intensity contribution function and its optical-depth-weighted differential for a single disk tile.\n\nApplies microturbulent broadening (with per-layer Doppler shift v_los), integrates the optical depth using either the anchored or Bézier scheme (as configured in mem), and evaluates the Planck-weighted contribution function at each layer boundary.\n\nArguments:\n\nαs_init::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Absorption coefficients, shape (Natm, Nλ).\natm::AtmosphereGPU: GPU atmosphere.\nmem::GPUMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working arrays.\ncmem::AbstractConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU convolution memory.\nμ_tile::Real: Cosine of the local zenith angle for this disk tile.\nv_los::CuArray{<:Real,1}: Per-layer line-of-sight velocity from rotation (m/s).\nv_mic::Union{T, CuArray{T,1}}: Microturbulent broadening width (m/s). Scalar for uniform broadening; CuVector for per-layer broadening.\n\nReturns:\n\nIntensityContFunc with fields:\ncfunc: Intensity contribution function C_I, shape (Natm-1, Nλ).\ncfunc_dt: cfunc .* Δτ, the differential contribution.\n\nSee also: calc_flux_quantities\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_intensity_quantities"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_intensity_quantities_broadened!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CUDACore.CuArray{T, 2}, FormationTemps.AtmosphereGPU{T}, FormationTemps.GPUMemory, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_intensity_quantities_broadened!(αs_b, atm, mem, μ_tile)\n\nIntensity contribution function and its optical-depth differential from opacities that are already microturbulence-broadened: calc_intensity_quantities without the convolution step.\n\nIntended for callers whose broadened opacities do not depend on μ_tile, so that one convolution serves every μ node. The ring quadrature is one: it applies its Doppler shift after the transfer solve.\n\nThe returned cfunc/cfunc_dt alias mem.cfunc/mem.cfunc_dt; consume them before the next call sharing mem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_intensity_quantities_broadened!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_stellar_grid-Union{Tuple{T1}, Tuple{T1, T1, T1, Int64}} where T1<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_stellar_grid(ρs, i, vsini, Nϕ)\n\nCompute a stellar surface grid on the GPU for disk integration. Geometry follows  that from  S. S. Vogt et al. (1987) and N. Piskunov & O. Kochukhov (2002). \n\nArguments:\n\nρs::Real: Stellar radius (in solar radii)\ni::Real: Inclination in degrees, in the range [-90, 90].\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity.\nNϕ::Int: Number of latitude bins; longitude bins vary with latitude.\n\nReturns:\n\nμs::CuArray: Cosine of the angle between the surface normal and the line of sight per tile.\ndA::CuArray: Projected surface area per tile.\nz_rot::CuArray: Line-of-sight rotational velocity per tile.\nz_cbs::CuArray: Additional per-tile velocity term. Disused in this implementation. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_stellar_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_tau_anchored_cpu!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_tau_anchored_cpu!(μ_i, τ_ref, α_ref, αs, τs)\n\nCompute optical depth by integrating in d(log τ_ref) rather than ds, following the \"anchored\" scheme of Korg (Wheeler et al. 2022, Appendix A.1).\n\nThe change of variables τ = ∫ α ds = ∫ (α/αref) dτref eliminates the sensitivity to non-uniform geometric layer spacing in the model atmosphere.\n\nα_ref must be the physical continuum absorption coefficient at the MARCS reference wavelength for each layer, computed from chemistry (not estimated from layer geometry). This is what makes the integration independent of Δz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_tau_anchored_cpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_tau_anchored_kernel!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CUDACore.CuDeviceMatrix{T}, CUDACore.CuDeviceMatrix{T}, CUDACore.CuDeviceVector{T}, CUDACore.CuDeviceVector{T}, T, Int32}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_tau_anchored_kernel!(αs, τs, log_τ_ref, ifactor_base, inv_μ, Nλ)\n\nGPU kernel for anchored τ integration.  One thread per wavelength; serial loop over N layers.\n\nIntegrates τ[i] = τ[i-1] + 0.5*(f[i-1]+f[i]) * Δ(log τref)[i] where f[k] = αs[k,j] * ifactorbase[k] * invμ  and  ifactorbase[k] = τref[k] / αref[k].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_tau_anchored_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.calc_tau_bezier_cpu!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"calc_tau_bezier_cpu!(μ_i, zs, αs, τs)\n\nCompute optical depth along a ray at μ_i by integrating α over geometric depth, with α interpolated by a quadratic Bézier curve per layer (de la Cruz Rodríguez & Piskunov 2013). Used when the atmosphere carries no τ_ref for the anchored scheme.\n\nNode derivatives are Fritsch-Butland weighted harmonic means, zero at local extrema. Each control point lies on its node's tangent line at the midpoint of the adjacent layer, which makes the layer integral the Hermite-corrected trapezoid; the two end layers have a single control point. Control points are clamped to [0, 2 max(α)] and τs[1, :] = TAU_FLOOR.\n\nMirrors calc_tau_bezier_cached_kernel! operation for operation, so the CPU and GPU paths agree to rounding. Korg.RadiativeTransfer.compute_tau_bezier! is not a substitute: its control points carry the opposite signs, and it neither zeroes the derivative at extrema nor uses the same clamp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.calc_tau_bezier_cpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.ceiling_ratio-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"ceiling_ratio(cfunc_dt, τ_ref) -> Vector\nceiling_ratio(result::FormTempResult) -> Vector\n\nPer-wavelength top-of-atmosphere contamination statistic: the topmost interval's flux contribution density as a fraction of the column peak density.\n\nNear 0 when the contribution function decays inside the model atmosphere, near 1 when it is still rising at the top layer, where the formation temperature is a lower limit set by where the model was truncated. Threshold with boundary_mask; also stored on FormTempResult.\n\nτ_ref is required because this compares two intervals: on the native MARCS grid the top interval is twice as wide as one in the finely-sampled region, which inflates a bare ratio of integrals by ~2× and over-flags. cfunc_per_dex divides that measure out.\n\nAn all-zero column yields 0, not NaN. form_temps_from_cfunc reports those wavelengths instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.ceiling_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.cfunc_per_dex-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"cfunc_per_dex(cfunc_dt, τ_ref) -> Matrix\ncfunc_per_dex(result::FormTempResult) -> Matrix\n\nConvert a contribution function from per-interval integrals to a density per dex of reference optical depth, dF / dlog₁₀τ_ref.\n\ncont_func holds integrals: sum(cont_func, dims=1) is the emergent flux, so the layer measure is baked into each element. Sums over it are therefore independent of the layer grid, but plotting it against depth, or comparing one interval against another, is not — the native MARCS grid changes spacing by 2× at log τ_ref = -3 and +1, which shows up as a step of the same size. Divide by the interval width first.\n\nUse this for plotting and for any across-layer comparison. Do not use it for weighted sums over depth: those need the integrals, where the measure cancels (see form_temps_from_cfunc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.cfunc_per_dex"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.component_rows-Tuple{StackedRenderMemory, Integer}","page":"Full Index","text":"component_rows(smem, b)\n\nRows of the stacked buffers of smem that hold component b: (b-1)*Natm+1 : b*Natm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.component_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.compute_alpha!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, Korg.Wavelengths, Any, Atmosphere{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AlphaCache{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"compute_alpha!(αs, wls, linelist, atm, A_X, cache; kwargs...)\ncompute_alpha!(αs, wls, linelist, zs, Ts, nds, nes, A_X, cache; kwargs...)\n\nCache-accelerated overloads of FormationTemps.compute_alpha!.\n\nThese overloads are additive: existing FormationTemps methods remain unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.compute_alpha!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.compute_alpha!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Korg.Wavelengths, Any, Atmosphere{T}, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"compute_alpha!(αs, wls, linelist, atm, A_X; partition_funcs=Korg.default_partition_funcs, ne_warn_thresh=0.1)\ncompute_alpha!(αs, wls, linelist, zs, Ts, nds, nes, A_X; partition_funcs=Korg.default_partition_funcs, ne_warn_thresh=0.1)\n\nCompute total (continuum + line) absorption coefficients in-place.\n\nArguments:\n\nαs::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Output array for absorption coefficients, sized (Nlayers, Nλ).\nwls::Korg.Wavelengths: Wavelength grid for the absorption calculation.\nlinelist: Line list passed to Korg.line_absorption!.\natm::Atmosphere or (zs, Ts, nds, nes): Atmospheric structure (heights, temperatures, number densities, electron densities).\nA_X::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Elemental abundances on the usual astronomical scale.\npartition_funcs=Korg.default_partition_funcs: Partition function table for chemical equilibrium.\nne_warn_thresh=0.1: Relative warning threshold for electron density updates.\nhydrogen_lines=true: Include hydrogen (Balmer/Brackett) lines, which Korg computes from dedicated Stark/MHD physics rather than from the linelist.\nhydrogen_line_window_size_Å=150.0: Per-line hydrogen window (matches Korg's default).\nuse_MHD=nothing: MHD occupation-probability formalism for the hydrogen lines. nothing enables it only below 13000 Å; pass true to reproduce Korg's default at all wavelengths. See _add_hydrogen_line_absorption!.\n\nReturns:\n\nnothing: αs is filled in-place.\n\nNotes:\n\nAdapted from Korg.line_absorption!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.compute_alpha!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.compute_rt_macro_dft_layout_2d!-NTuple{7, Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"compute_rt_macro_dft_layout_2d!(kbuf, xs, v_losals, i0, ζ_rt, Nλ, L)\ncompute_rt_macro_dft_layout_2d!(kbuf, dx, λ0, v_losals, i0, ζ_rt, Nλ, L)\n\nCUDA kernel: evaluate the RT macroturbulence kernel for multiple μ values in parallel, writing directly in DFT layout (zero-lag at index L). Each row of kbuf (N_unique, L) corresponds to one unique μ value. Skips the roll + ifftshift needed by the serial path.\n\nThe first form differences the device grid xs. The second takes the offsets dx[j] = xs[j] - xs[i0] and the center wavelength λ0 from the host, where they can be differenced from the grid at the precision it was generated at (see _kernel_offsets).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.compute_rt_macro_dft_layout_2d!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.conv_kernel_vmax-Tuple{Real, Real, Real}","page":"Full Index","text":"conv_kernel_vmax(vsini, ζ, ξ) -> Float64\n\nLine-of-sight velocity half-support (m/s) bounding every kernel convolved along the wavelength axis: rotation displaces the microturbulent kernel by up to vsini, the radial-tangential macro kernel reaches ~3ζ, and the microturbulent Gaussian reaches ~3ξ. ξ may be a scalar or a per-layer vector.\n\nSums the three rather than taking the max, so one padding geometry bounds them whether they are applied together or separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.conv_kernel_vmax"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.conv_npad_for_velocity-Tuple{Real, Real, Real}","page":"Full Index","text":"conv_npad_for_velocity(λ0, Δλ, vmax; margin=64, minpad=512) -> Int\n\nMinimum Npad for which a padded linear convolution with kernel half-support vmax (m/s) does not wrap. λ0 and Δλ are the grid center and spacing (Å).\n\n_conv_mem_geometry splits Npad evenly between the two edges, so a kernel reaching h pixels needs Npad ≥ 2h. Under-padding does not raise: with pad_left < h the valid-region extraction pulls samples that have wrapped from the opposite edge, with no NaN and no warning. The half-width vmax/Δv grows as Δλ shrinks, so a vsini that is safe on a coarse grid can wrap on a fine one.\n\nminpad floors the result, so padding is only ever added. Extra padding appends zeros to the kernel, leaving results unchanged wherever the floor already sufficed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.conv_npad_for_velocity"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_gray_rotation(xs, ys, vsini, u1)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the Gray (2008) rotation kernel using linear limb darkening.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum with the same shape as ys.\n\nSee also: gray_rot_kernel, convolve_gray_rotation_gpu\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_gray_rotation_gpu(cmem, xs, ys, vsini, u1)\n\nGPU implementation of convolve_gray_rotation. Convolves each row of ys with the Gray (2008) rotation kernel using padded FFT convolution on the device.\n\nArguments:\n\ncmem::MacroConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working memory.\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Input matrix with shape (Natm, Nλ).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nxs_exact: xs at the precision it was generated at; the kernel is placed from its differences (see _kernel_offsets). Defaults to xs itself.\n\nReturns:\n\nout::CuArray{<:Real,2}: Convolved matrix on the GPU, same shape as ys.\n\nNotes:\n\nCPU and GPU both use padded linear convolution with edge replication.\n\nSee also: convolve_gray_rotation, gray_rot_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_gray_rotation_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, Vararg{T, 4}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_hirano_rotmacro(xs, ys, vsini, ζ_rt, u1, u2; intres=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the Hirano et al. (2011) combined rotation+macroturbulence kernel. The kernel is computed analytically in the Fourier domain and applied via FFT.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nu2::Real: Quadratic limb-darkening coefficient.\nintres::Int=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS: Number of quadrature points for the disk integral.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum with the same shape as ys.\n\nSee also: hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel, convolve_hirano_rotmacro_gpu\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, Vararg{T, 4}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_hirano_rotmacro_gpu(cmem, xs, ys, vsini, ζ_rt, u1, u2; intres=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS)\n\nGPU implementation of convolve_hirano_rotmacro. Convolves each row of ys with the Hirano et al. (2011) combined rotation+macroturbulence kernel using padded FFT convolution on the device.\n\nArguments:\n\ncmem::MacroConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working memory.\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Input matrix with shape (Natm, Nλ).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nu2::Real: Quadratic limb-darkening coefficient.\nintres::Int=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS: Quadrature resolution for the kernel integral.\n\nReturns:\n\nout::CuArray{<:Real,2}: Convolved matrix on the GPU, same shape as ys.\n\nNotes:\n\nShort-circuits (returns CuArray(ys)) when both vsini and ζ_rt are zero.\nThe Hirano FT kernel is computed entirely on the GPU via hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel_gpu!, then converted to the spatial domain and applied via padded FFT convolution on the device.\nCPU and GPU both use padded linear convolution with edge replication.\n\nSee also: convolve_hirano_rotmacro, hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel_gpu!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_hirano_rotmacro_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_instrument_gauss-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_instrument_gauss(xs, ys; new_res=1.17e5, oversampling=2.0)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with a Gaussian LSF at resolving power new_res.  The output spectrum is resampled onto a new wavelength grid (evenly spaced in log-wavelength, i.e., velocity)  at the specified oversampling factor. \n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Input wavelength grid.\nys::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Input spectrum sampled on xs.\nnew_res::Real=1.17e5: Target resolving power (λ/Δλ) for the Gaussian LSF.\noversampling::Real=2.0: Oversampling factor for the output log-wavelength grid.\n\nReturns:\n\nxs_out::Vector{<:Real}: Output wavelength grid at the requested resolution.\nys_out::Vector{<:Real}: Spectrum convolved with the Gaussian LSF and rebinned to xs_out.\n\nSee also: rebin_spectrum\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_instrument_gauss"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_iso_rt_macro(xs, ys, ζ_rt)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the isotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.8). This is the disk-integrated (μ-averaged) form appropriate for stellar flux spectra.\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nζ_rt::Real: Isotropic macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum (or ys if ζ_rt == 0).\n\nSee also: gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel, convolve_iso_rt_macro_gpu, convolve_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_iso_rt_macro_gpu(cmem, xs, ys, ζ_rt)\n\nGPU implementation of convolve_iso_rt_macro. Convolves each row of ys with the isotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel using padded FFT convolution on the device.\n\nArguments:\n\ncmem::MacroConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working memory.\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Input matrix with shape (Natm, Nλ).\nζ_rt::Real: Isotropic macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nxs_exact: xs at the precision it was generated at; the kernel is placed from its differences (see _kernel_offsets). Defaults to xs itself.\n\nReturns:\n\nout::CuArray{<:Real,2}: Convolved matrix on the GPU, same shape as ys.\n\nNotes:\n\nShort-circuits (returns CuArray(ys)) when ζ_rt is zero.\nCPU and GPU both use padded linear convolution with edge replication.\n\nSee also: convolve_iso_rt_macro, gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_iso_rt_macro_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_rt_macro(xs, ys, ζ_rt, μ)\n\nConvolve a spectrum with the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6), assuming equal radial and tangential velocity scales (ζ_R = ζ_T).\n\nArguments:\n\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Spectrum on xs (vector or matrix with rows as spectra).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nys_out::AbstractArray{<:Real}: Convolved spectrum (or ys if ζ_rt == 0).\n\nSee also: rt_macro_kernel, convolve_iso_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_rt_macro_gpu(cmem, xs, ys, ζ_r, ζ_t, μ)\n\nGPU implementation of convolve_rt_macro(xs, ys, ζ_r, ζ_t, μ). Convolves each row of ys with the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel using padded FFT convolution on the device, allowing independent radial and tangential velocity scales.\n\nArguments:\n\ncmem::MacroConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working memory.\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Input matrix with shape (Natm, Nλ).\nζ_r::Real: Radial macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nζ_t::Real: Tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\nxs_exact: xs at the precision it was generated at; the kernel is placed from its differences (see _kernel_offsets). Defaults to xs itself.\n\nReturns:\n\nout::CuArray{<:Real,2}: Convolved matrix on the GPU, same shape as ys.\n\nNotes:\n\nShort-circuits (returns CuArray(ys)) when both ζ_r and ζ_t are zero.\nCPU and GPU both use padded linear convolution with edge replication.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro, rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_rt_macro_gpu(cmem, xs, ys, ζ_rt, μ)\n\nGPU implementation of convolve_rt_macro. Convolves each row of ys with the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel using padded FFT convolution on the device.\n\nArguments:\n\ncmem::MacroConvolutionMemory: Pre-allocated GPU working memory.\nxs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Wavelength grid (Å).\nys::AbstractMatrix{<:Real}: Input matrix with shape (Natm, Nλ).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\nxs_exact: xs at the precision it was generated at; the kernel is placed from its differences (see _kernel_offsets). Defaults to xs itself.\n\nReturns:\n\nout::CuArray{<:Real,2}: Convolved matrix on the GPU, same shape as ys.\n\nNotes:\n\nShort-circuits (returns CuArray(ys)) when ζ_rt is zero.\nCPU and GPU both use padded linear convolution with edge replication.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro, rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractMatrix{T}, CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 1}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached(cmem, ys, kernel_ft)\n\nConvolve ys with a precomputed RT macroturbulence kernel FFT. Skips kernel computation entirely. Use with precompute_rt_macro_kernel_ft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_wavelength_axis-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_wavelength_axis(xs, ys, v_los, v_mic)\n\nConvolve each row of ys with a Gaussian kernel that models microturbulent broadening and a Doppler shift, using FFT convolution. The kernel width is wavelength-dependent (constant in velocity units): σ(x) = x·v_mic/c.\n\nScalar v_los and v_mic apply the same kernel to every row; vectors specify per-row values. The GPU implementation (convolve_wavelength_axis_gpu) builds the same kernel on device, so CPU and GPU agree to floating-point precision.\n\nPadding is sized from the v_los/v_mic passed, so a large Doppler shift cannot wrap the padded linear convolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_wavelength_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.convolve_wavelength_axis_batched!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.BatchedMicroConvMem{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractMatrix{T}, CUDACore.CuArray{T, 1}, T, Int64}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"convolve_wavelength_axis_batched!(bcmem, xs, ys, v_los_batch, v_mic, Bcur)\n\nBatched Doppler convolution for Bcur tiles simultaneously. The absorption signal ys (Natm × Nλ) is shared across tiles; v_los_batch (BcurNatm) provides per-tile velocities. Scalar v_mic avoids tiling allocations; vector v_mic is tiled across tiles. Returns a view of the valid region `(BcurNatm, Nλ)`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.convolve_wavelength_axis_batched!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.elav-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Full Index","text":"elav(a::AbstractVector)\nelav(a; dims)\n\nCompute midpoints between adjacent elements along dims.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.elav"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.form_temps_from_cfunc-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"form_temps_from_cfunc(cfunc_dt, Ts; τ_ref=T[], r_thresh=BOUNDARY_R_THRESH,\n                      warn_boundary=true) -> Vector\n\nFormation temperature at 50% of the cumulative flux contribution.\n\nThe cumulative is node-anchored: F = 0 at the top node Ts[1], and the contribution accumulated through interval k is assigned to the deep node Ts[k+1], so the 50% crossing interpolates against node temperatures Ts. Interval-center temperatures (elav(Ts)) would bias the result half an interval cool.\n\ncfunc_dt is (Natm-1, Nλ) (per-interval flux contribution); Ts is (Natm,) node temperatures. CPU; GPU callers pass host copies.\n\ncfunc_dt must be the per-interval integrals, not a density: the cumulative sum is what makes this independent of the layer grid, because each element already carries its own interval width. Passing cfunc_per_dex output here would drop that weighting.\n\nTwo degenerate cases warn:\n\nNon-positive column total: no median exists, so the formation temperature is NaN. Reachable when an upstream microturbulence kernel underflows and zeros a column.\nBoundary contamination: where ceiling_ratio exceeds r_thresh the contribution is still peaking at the top layer, so the formation temperature is a lower limit near Ts[1]. Flags the same wavelengths as boundary_mask at that r_thresh; warn_boundary=false silences it.\n\nτ_ref is used only by that second warning, which compares intervals and so needs the layer measure. Omitting it falls back to a uniform measure, matching what ceiling_ratio does when no reference grid exists, so the warned set always equals the masked set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.form_temps_from_cfunc"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.get_Ts-Tuple{Atmosphere}","page":"Full Index","text":"get_Ts(atm)\n\nReturn the temperature grid as a standard Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_Ts"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.get_cum_cfunc-Tuple{FormationTemps.ContFunc}","page":"Full Index","text":"get_cum_cfunc(cfunc::ContFunc) -> Matrix\n\nCumulative contribution curve for plotting: an (Natm-1, Nλ) cell-edge cumulative, max-normalized per column.\n\nNot a CDF. It has no top-node anchor and is normalized by the column maximum rather than the total, so inverting it at 0.5 against interval-center temperatures gives a formation temperature biased half an interval cool. Use form_temps_from_cfunc instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_cum_cfunc"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.get_nd-Tuple{Atmosphere}","page":"Full Index","text":"get_nd(atm)\n\nReturn the number density grid as a standard Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_nd"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.get_zs-Tuple{Atmosphere}","page":"Full Index","text":"get_zs(atm)\n\nReturn the height grid as a standard Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_zs"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.get_τs-Tuple{Atmosphere}","page":"Full Index","text":"get_τs(atm)\n\nReturn the optical depth reference grid as a standard Array. Returns an empty vector when the atmosphere was constructed from a model that does not supply tau_ref (in that case the Bézier τ integrator is used and atm.τs is empty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.get_τs"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_rt)\n\nCompute the isotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.8), assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT) and equal velocity scales (ζR = ζT). The kernel is the disk-integrated (μ-averaged) form.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid centered on the line core (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Isotropic macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_iso_rt_macro, rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.gray_rot_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"gray_rot_kernel(vs, vsini, u1)\n\nCompute the Gray (2008) rotation broadening kernel (Eq. 18.14) with linear limb darkening.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid centered on the line core (m/s).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nu1::Real: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized rotation kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_gray_rotation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.gray_rot_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel(σs, vsini, ζ_rt; u1=0.43, u2=0.31, intres=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS)\n\nCompute the Fourier transform of the Hirano et al. (2011) rotation+macroturbulence kernel (Eq. B12) by quadrature integration over the visible stellar disk.\n\nArguments:\n\nσs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Frequency grid (s/m; inverse of velocity units).\nvsini::Real: Projected rotational velocity (m/s).\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nu1::Real=0.43: Linear limb-darkening coefficient.\nu2::Real=0.31: Quadratic limb-darkening coefficient.\nintres::Int=HIRANO_QUADRATURE_NPTS: Number of quadrature points for the disk integral.\n\nReturns:\n\nKσ::Vector{<:Real}: Fourier transform of the combined kernel, same length as σs.\n\nSee also: convolve_hirano_rotmacro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel_gpu!-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel_gpu!(Kσ, σs, vsini, ζ_rt, u1, u2, intres, Nσ)\n\nCUDA kernel that computes the Fourier transform of the Hirano et al. (2011) rotation+macroturbulence kernel directly on the GPU. One block per frequency bin; threads within each block split the intres quadrature points and reduce via shared memory.\n\nThis is the GPU equivalent of hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.hirano_rotmacro_ft_kernel_gpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.precompute_rt_macro_kernel_ft-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormationTemps.MacroConvolutionMemory, AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"precompute_rt_macro_kernel_ft(cmem, xs, ζ_rt, μ; xs_exact=nothing)\n\nPrecompute the Fourier transform of the RT macroturbulence kernel for a given μ. Returns a CuVector{Complex{T}} that can be passed to convolve_rt_macro_gpu_cached. xs_exact is xs at the precision it was generated at; the kernel is placed from its differences (see _kernel_offsets).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.precompute_rt_macro_kernel_ft"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.rebin_spectrum-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"rebin_spectrum(xs_old, ys_old, xs_new)\nrebin_spectrum(xs_old, ys_old, σs_old, xs_new)\n\nRebin a spectrum (and optional uncertainties) from xs_old to xs_new.\n\nBased on the SpectRes spectral resampling implementation: https://github.com/ACCarnall/SpectRes/blob/master/spectres/spectral_resampling.py\n\nNote: the algorithm may introduce small shifts if the wavelength grids are misaligned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.rebin_spectrum"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.render_stacked!-Union{Tuple{StackedRenderMemory{T, V, M, MI, C, PF, PB} where {V<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 1}), M<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 2}), MI<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 2}), C<:(CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}), PF<:AbstractFFTs.Plan{T}, PB<:AbstractFFTs.Plan{Complex{T}}}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"render_stacked!(smem)\n\nRender every component of smem at the velocities in smem.v_los and smem.v_mic (m/s, length B*Natm) and write the emergent intensities to smem.intensity (Nλ × B).\n\nColumn b of smem.intensity is bit-identical to the serial render of component b (calc_intensity_cfunc! with its signal FFT cached, then reduce_intensity!) on its own GPUMemory and ConvolutionMemory, the mems[b] and cmems[b] that smem was built from. Every one of those ConvolutionMemorys must have rendered at least once before the first call, which is what sets its wavelength grid, kernel offsets and σ_floor; the first call copies them into smem, and no later call reads a ConvolutionMemory. Every component's signal FFT must have been handed over with set_signal_ft!.\n\nAfterwards the valid columns of smem.conv_gpu hold the convolved opacities and smem.τs and smem.cfunc the optical depths and contribution functions, in the stacked row layout.\n\nAllocates no device memory on a call after the first whose underflow check is not due.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.render_stacked!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.reset_alpha_cache!-Tuple{AlphaCache}","page":"Full Index","text":"reset_alpha_cache!(cache)\n\nDrop warm-start state while keeping allocated buffers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.reset_alpha_cache!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.round_to_power-Tuple{Real}","page":"Full Index","text":"round_to_power(x::Real)\n\nRound x to one significant digit based on its order of magnitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.round_to_power"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_r, ζ_t, μ)\n\nCompute the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6) with independent radial and tangential velocity scales, assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT). The radial and the tangential Gaussian are each normalized over the samples of vs, so both carry the weight 1/2 even where one is narrower than a pixel.\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid (m/s), uniform, with a sample at zero velocity.\nζ_r::Real: Radial macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nζ_t::Real: Tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_rt, μ)\n\nCompute the anisotropic radial-tangential macroturbulence kernel from Gray (2008) (Eq. 17.6), assuming equal amplitudes (AR = AT) and equal velocity scales (ζR = ζT). The radial and the tangential Gaussian are each normalized over the samples of vs, so both carry the weight 1/2 even where one is narrower than a pixel (μ → 1, μ → 0).\n\nArguments:\n\nvs::AbstractVector{<:Real}: Velocity grid (m/s), uniform, with a sample at zero velocity.\nζ_rt::Real: Radial-tangential macroturbulence velocity scale (m/s).\nμ::Real: Cosine of the angle between the local normal and the line of sight.\n\nReturns:\n\nkernel::Vector{<:Real}: Normalized macroturbulence kernel evaluated on vs.\n\nSee also: convolve_rt_macro, gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.rt_macro_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.searchsortednearest-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, T}} where T","page":"Full Index","text":"searchsortednearest(a, x)\nsearchsortednearest(x, a)\n\nReturn the index of the element in sorted vector a that is closest to x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.searchsortednearest"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.set_abundances!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AlphaCache{T}, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"set_abundances!(cache, A_X)\n\nRefresh cached absolute abundances after changing A_X.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.set_abundances!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.set_signal_ft!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{StackedRenderMemory{T, V, M, MI, C, PF, PB} where {V<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 1}), M<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 2}), MI<:(CUDACore.CuArray{T, 2}), C<:(CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}), PF<:AbstractFFTs.Plan{T}, PB<:AbstractFFTs.Plan{Complex{T}}}, Integer, CUDACore.CuArray{Complex{T}, 2}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Full Index","text":"set_signal_ft!(smem, b, signal_ft)\n\nCopy the padded-signal FFT of component b into smem. signal_ft is (Natm, nfreq): what cmem.signal_ft_gpu holds after a serial render of that component's opacities on its own ConvolutionMemory, the cmems[b] that smem was built from. Call it again whenever the component's opacities change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.set_signal_ft!"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.vmac_fit-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"vmac_fit(teff, logg)\nvmac_fit(teff)\n\nEmpirical macroturbulent velocity fits.\n\nvmac_fit(teff, logg) uses the Doyle et al. (2014) relation with teff in K and logg in cgs. vmac_fit(teff) uses the Bruntt et al. (2010) relation with teff in K. Both return the macroturbulent velocity in m/s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.vmac_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"longlist/#FormationTemps.vmic_fit-Tuple{Any}","page":"Full Index","text":"vmic_fit(teff)\n\nEmpirical microturbulent velocity fit from Bruntt et al. (2010).\n\nInput teff is in K; returns microturbulent velocity in m/s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FormationTemps.vmic_fit"},{"category":"section","location":"license/#License","page":"License","text":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2025 Michael Palumbo\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n\n","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Parallelization","page":"Parallelization","text":"FormationTemps.jl can use CPU threads and an NVIDIA GPU. What each speeds up depends on the integration method:\n\nmethod CPU threads GPU\n:disk surface tiles run in parallel yes\n:quadrature absorption calculation only yes\n:hirano absorption calculation only yes\n\n:disk is the method that needs parallel hardware most. :quadrature is fast enough that the absorption calculation is often the largest part of a call.","title":"Parallelization"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#CPU-multithreading","page":"Parallelization","text":"Start Julia with several threads:\n\njulia -t auto           # all available cores\njulia -t 8              # 8 threads\n\nor set JULIA_NUM_THREADS before launching. :disk on the CPU scales well to about 16 threads (about 12× at 16).\n\nwarning: Python interoperability\nMultithreading is not compatible with calling FormationTemps.jl from Python through juliacall: it can crash the process. Set JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1 when calling from Python, and use the GPU or method=:quadrature for speed instead. See the Python Tutorial.","title":"CPU multithreading"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#GPU-acceleration","page":"Parallelization","text":"The GPU is used by default when a working NVIDIA device is found (CUDA.functional() == true); pass use_gpu=false to force the CPU. To check your setup:\n\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.functional()   # should return true\nCUDA.versioninfo()  # device and toolkit details\n\nIf CUDA.functional() returns false, see the CUDA.jl troubleshooting guide; FormationTemps.jl falls back to the CPU.","title":"GPU acceleration"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Precision","page":"Parallelization","text":"result = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist; gpu_precision=Float32)\n\ngpu_precision=Float32 roughly halves GPU memory and can be faster on consumer GPUs. Absorption is always computed at Float64 and converted. The cost in accuracy is small (see below). The default is Float64.","title":"Precision"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Benchmarks","page":"Parallelization","text":"Measured on an Intel Xeon w5-3435X (16 cores / 32 threads) with an NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada (48 GB), for two Fe I lines near 6300 Å with solar parameters (vsini = 2100 m/s, ζ = 3500 m/s, ξ = 850 m/s) and Nϕ = 128. Regenerate everything with julia --project=. -t auto benchmarks/run_all.jl.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Cost-per-surface-tile-(:disk)","page":"Parallelization","text":"(Image: per-tile benchmark)","title":"Cost per surface tile (:disk)"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#CPU-thread-scaling-(:disk)","page":"Parallelization","text":"(Image: threading benchmark)","title":"CPU thread scaling (:disk)"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Performance-vs.-wavelength-grid-size-(:disk)","page":"Parallelization","text":"(Image: Nlambda benchmark)","title":"Performance vs. wavelength grid size (:disk)"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Broadening-kernels","page":"Parallelization","text":"Time per broadening kernel on CPU and GPU.\n\n(Image: convolution benchmark)","title":"Broadening kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Tips","page":"Parallelization","text":"GPU memory. Each call allocates its GPU buffers. If memory is tight, use gpu_precision=Float32.\nLarge linelists. The absorption calculation runs on the CPU for every method and grows with the number of lines × Nλ. For large linelists it can dominate the total time, GPU or not; see Large Linelists.","title":"Tips"},{"category":"section","location":"parallelization/#Float32-vs.-Float64-accuracy","page":"Parallelization","text":"On the CPU and the GPU at Float64 the results agree to rounding. At Float32 the GPU results differ from the CPU by about 10⁻⁶ in flux and a few mK in formation temperature with :disk, and by about 10⁻⁵ and 0.1 K with :hirano. The figures show both, for two Fe I lines near 6300 Å; the bottom rows are residuals against the CPU at Float64.\n\n(Image: GPU precision: convolution path)\n\n(Image: GPU precision: disk integration)","title":"Float32 vs. Float64 accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Basic-Tutorial","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"In the simplest use case, a model flux spectrum and associated formation temperatures can be calculated given stellar parameters as input. For further details on specifying linelists, abundances, and other details of the model atmosphere, please see the Korg.jl documentation.\n\nusing Korg\nusing PythonPlot; plt = PythonPlot.pyplot\nusing FormationTemps; FT = FormationTemps\nplt.style.use(joinpath(FT.moddir, \"fig.mplstyle\"))\n\n# get the linelist\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[16000:16100]\n\n# set stellar parameters\nTeff = 5777.0\nlogg = 4.44\nFe_H = 0.0\nvsini = 2100.0\nζ_RT = 3400.0   # radial-tangential macroturbulent broadening \nξ = 850.0       # microturbulent broadening\n\n# create StellarProps composite type to hold everything \nstar_props = StellarProps(Teff=Teff, logg=logg, Fe_H=Fe_H, \n                          vsini=vsini, v_macro=ζ_RT, v_micro=ξ)\n\n# get the flux + formation temperature spectra\nform_temp_result = FT.calc_formation_temp(star_props, linelist; Δλ=0.01)\n\n# parse the result\nwavs = form_temp_result.wavs\nflux = form_temp_result.flux\ntemp = form_temp_result.form_temps\n\n# plot the result\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6,4.8))\nax1.plot(wavs, temp, c=\"k\")\nax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Vacuum Wavelength [\\\\AA]}\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Formation Temperature [K]}\")\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"temp_example_jl.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n(Image: formation_temps)\n\nThe high-level convenience function calc_formation_temp provides a few optional arguments discussed below.","title":"Basic Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#StellarProps","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"v_micro (microturbulent velocity ξ) accepts either a scalar or a vector of length Natm for per-layer microturbulence. All other velocity parameters (vsini, v_macro) are scalars. vsini is the projected equatorial rotational velocity.\n\nRotation geometry is set by istar (inclination in degrees; 90 = equator-on) and the differential-rotation coefficients α₂, α₄ in the normalized rate law Ω(ϕ)/Ω_eq = 1 - α₂·sin²ϕ - α₄·sin⁴ϕ (default 0, i.e. solid-body). For rigid rotation the broadening depends only on vsini, so istar has no effect; with differential rotation (α ≠ 0) istar selects which latitude bands are visible and therefore matters. See Integration Methods.","title":"StellarProps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Air-and-vacuum-wavelengths","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"Korg computes in vacuum, always, and Korg.read_linelist converts an air-wavelength file to vacuum as it reads it. FormationTemps.jl builds its wavelength grid from the linelist, so the wavs we get back are vacuum Angstroms. If we want to present air wavelengths, we convert the output axis:\n\nwavs_air = Korg.vacuum_to_air.(result.wavs)\n\nwarning: Do not convert by rewriting the linelist\nIt is tempting to shift the linelist itself, e.g. Korg.Line(l, wl=Korg.vacuum_to_air(l.wl)). Doing so tells Korg that each metal line's vacuum wavelength is its air value, so the metals move — but the hydrogen lines do not, because Korg builds those from its own tabulated data rather than from the linelist. The result is a spectrum whose metal and hydrogen lines sit roughly 1.8 Angstroms apart at H-alpha, with no error or warning to indicate it. Air is a labelling of the sample points, not a frame the physics runs in.","title":"Air and vacuum wavelengths"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Hydrogen-lines","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"Hydrogen lines are included by default. Korg computes them from dedicated Stark-broadening physics rather than from the linelist, so they appear whether or not the linelist contains any hydrogen transitions. Pass hydrogen_lines=false to calc_formation_temp to leave them out. hydrogen_line_window_size_Å (default 150 Å, as in Korg) sets how far from each line center their opacity is computed.\n\nOne default differs from Korg's. The MHD occupation-probability formalism (use_MHD) is switched on only when the whole wavelength range lies below 13000 Å, following Korg's own advice for the near-infrared. Pass use_MHD=true to match Korg.synthesize there.","title":"Hydrogen lines"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Convolution-vs.-Integration","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"At low spectral resolving power, convolutions can be used to approximate the effects of macroturbulent and rotational broadening. As shown in Section 2.1.4 of the paper presenting FormationTemps.jl, this approximation can fail at higher resolution. By default, calc_formation_temp performs an explicit disk integration (method=:disk) to evaluate model spectra and formation temperatures. Though more accurate, this approach is slower. To use the convolution approximation, pass method=:hirano (equivalently the legacy convolve=true) to calc_formation_temp. A faster middle ground, method=:quadrature, keeps most of the disk-integration accuracy; see Integration Methods. A plot comparing the convolution and integration fluxes and temperatures is shown below for a solar-like model star. As shown in the paper, the error incurred by the convolution approximation grows with v sin i.\n\n(Image: convolution_vs_integration)","title":"Convolution vs. Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Running-in-parallel","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"Disk integration (method=:disk, the default) is the most computationally intensive mode and benefits from parallelization on both CPU and GPU.","title":"Running in parallel"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#CPU-multithreading","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"The CPU disk integration path distributes tiles across Julia threads. Launch Julia with multiple threads to benefit:\n\njulia -t auto           # use all available cores\njulia -t 8              # use 8 threads\n\nNo code changes are required — calc_formation_temp detects the available threads automatically.\n\nwarning: Python interop\nCPU multithreading is not compatible with calling FormationTemps.jl from Python via juliacall/PythonCall. Set JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1 when calling from Python. Use the GPU path for parallelism in that case.","title":"CPU multithreading"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#GPU-acceleration","page":"Basic Tutorial","text":"By default, calc_formation_temp will use an NVIDIA GPU to accelerate the computation of spectra, if one is present and configured. The CUDA.jl documentation provides installation instructions. Pass use_gpu=false to force the CPU path. Pass gpu_precision=Float32 to run GPU computations at single precision, which roughly halves GPU memory and can improve throughput on consumer GPUs. Absorption coefficients are always computed at Float64 (a Korg requirement) and converted before GPU upload.\n\nFor details on what gets accelerated, memory layout, benchmarks, and CPU/GPU numerical differences, see the Parallelization guide.","title":"GPU acceleration"},{"category":"section","location":"#FormationTemps.jl","page":"Quickstart","text":"(Image: Stable) (Image: Dev) (Image: Build Status) (Image: arXiv)\n\nFormationTemps.jl wraps Korg.jl to produce model spectra and formation temperatures given fundamental stellar parameters and a linelist as input. We encourage users to read the paper that presents FormationTemps.jl. The scripts used to generate the plots and other quantitative results presented therein can be found in the scripts/ directory of the GitHub repo.","title":"FormationTemps.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Quickstart","text":"FormationTemps.jl is written entirely in Julia and requires Julia v1.12 or greater. Installation instructions for Julia are available from julialang.org.\n\nTo install FormationTemps.jl from the Julia REPL:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([\"FormationTemps\", \"Korg\"])\nusing FormationTemps\n\nKorg.jl is added alongside because linelists and abundances are read with Korg's own functions. FormationTemps depends on Korg, but Julia only loads packages that are direct dependencies of your active environment, so using Korg fails unless you add it yourself.\n\nAlternatively, if you'd like to tinker with the source of FormationTemps.jl, you can clone the repo from GitHub and add it in develop mode (adding Korg as above):\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(path=\"/path/to/FormationTemps.jl\")\nPkg.add(\"Korg\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Basic-Julia-Example","page":"Quickstart","text":"To compute a basic formation temperature spectrum (the plotting lines use PythonPlot.jl, so run Pkg.add(\"PythonPlot\") first if you want them as written):\n\nusing Korg\nimport PythonPlot; plt = PythonPlot\nusing FormationTemps; FT = FormationTemps\n\n# get the linelist\nlinelist = Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FT.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[16000:16100]\n\n# set stellar parameters\nTeff = 5777.0\nlogg = 4.44\nA_X = Korg.asplund_2020_solar_abundances\nFe_H = 0.0\nvsini = 2100.0\nζ_RT = 3400.0   # radial-tangential macroturbulent broadening\nξ = 850.0       # microturbulent broadening\n\n# create StellarProps composite type to hold everything\nstar_props = StellarProps(Teff=Teff, logg=logg, Fe_H=Fe_H,\n                          vsini=vsini, v_macro=ζ_RT, v_micro=ξ)\n\n# get the flux + formation temperature spectra (default: explicit disk integration).\n# pass method=:quadrature for a much faster, near-identical result, or method=:hirano\n# (with u1, u2) for the fast analytic convolution — see the \"Integration Methods\" docs.\nform_temp_result = FT.calc_formation_temp(star_props, linelist; Δλ=0.01)\n\n# parse the result\nwavs = form_temp_result.wavs\nflux = form_temp_result.flux\ntemp = form_temp_result.form_temps\n\n# plot the result\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots()\nax1.plot(wavs, temp, c=\"k\")\nax1.set_xlabel(\"Vacuum Wavelength [Å]\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"Formation Temperature [K]\")\nplt.show()\n\n(Image: formation_temps)\n\nIn strong saturated line cores the flux contribution can still be rising where the model atmosphere is truncated, which biases the formation temperature toward the top boundary. The result carries a ceiling_ratio statistic for this, and boundary_mask flags the affected wavelengths, so they can be excluded before interpreting a spectrum:\n\ngood = .!boundary_mask(form_temp_result)\nax1.plot(wavs[good], temp[good], c=\"k\")\n\nMore detail on the above example can be found in the Basic Tutorial and the high-level API documentation.","title":"Basic Julia Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Calling-FormationTemps.jl-from-Python","page":"Quickstart","text":"warning: Warning\nCalling FormationTemps.jl from Python is currently somewhat fragile; if you experience any problems, please open an Issue.\n\nFormationTemps.jl can be called from Python. The instructions can be found in the Python Tutorial.","title":"Calling FormationTemps.jl from Python"},{"category":"section","location":"#Parallelization-Overview","page":"Quickstart","text":"FormationTemps.jl parallelizes the disk integration pipeline in two ways. The CPU path distributes stellar surface tiles across Julia threads (julia -t auto); the GPU path offloads the full per-tile radiative transfer to an NVIDIA GPU via CUDA.jl. GPU acceleration is enabled automatically when a compatible device is detected; pass use_gpu=false to force the CPU path.\n\nTile threading applies to the default method=:disk. method=:quadrature has no tile loop to distribute — its speedup comes from solving the radiative transfer once per μ node instead of once per surface tile — so on CPU it threads only inside the absorption calculation. See the Parallelization documentation for setup, benchmarks, and limitations.","title":"Parallelization Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Caveats","page":"Quickstart","text":"danger: Danger\nUsers should be aware of the technical and \"philosophical\" discussion on formation temperatures in Sections 4.2 and 4.3 of the paper presenting FormationTemps.jl.\n\nIn brief:\n\nFormation temperatures are modeled and not measured quantities\nThe definition/concept of a formation temperature can belie some realities of radiative transfer (see the contribution function comparison in the relevant tutorial)\nKorg.jl only assumes LTE, and the MARCS model atmospheres used by default do not have chromospheres — a curated list of lines this renders untrustworthy ships in data/bad_lines.csv (see the relevant tutorial)\nThe model atmospheres are 1D, and do not handle the effects of convection (limb shift, line asymmetry, etc.) or magnetism","title":"Caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Quickstart","text":"(Image: DOI) (Image: arXiv)\n\nIf you use FormationTemps.jl in your research, please cite the relevant software release and paper. The cffconvert tool can be used to generate a bibtex entry from the included CITATION.cff (or just use the \"cite this repository\" button on the GitHub sidebar).","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Author-and-Contact","page":"Quickstart","text":"(Image: GitHub followers)\n\nThis repo is maintained by Michael Palumbo. You may may contact him via his email - mpalumbo@flatironinstitute.org","title":"Author & Contact"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Python-Tutorial","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"FormationTemps.jl can be called from Python using juliacall. This requires a working Julia installation (v1.12+) and Python 3.12+ with uv (recommended) or pip. We strongly advise against Conda.","title":"Python Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Prerequisites","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"Julia 1.12+: install from julialang.org or via juliaup. The install steps below ask juliapkg for Julia 1.12; if the julia on your PATH is newer, juliapkg prints a warning and uses it anyway, which works with 1.13.\nPython 3.12+ with uv (recommended) or pip.\nA LaTeX installation, to run the example below as written: it uses the package's plot style, which renders text with LaTeX.","title":"Prerequisites"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Installing-the-Python-interface","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"","title":"Installing the Python interface"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#From-a-Local-Clone","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"If you have cloned the FormationTemps.jl repository:\n\ncd FormationTemps.jl\nuv sync\nuv run python -c \"import ssl; print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\"   # must be 3.5 or newer\nuv run python deps/setup.py\n\nnote: If OpenSSL is older than 3.5\nJulia 1.12 can only be loaded into a Python linked against OpenSSL 3.5 or newer. Older uv releases install Pythons with OpenSSL 3.0; update uv and its Python, then rebuild the environment:uv self update            # or update uv the way you installed it\nuv python upgrade 3.12\nrm -rf .venv && uv syncAny other Python 3.12+ that qualifies also works: uv sync --python /path/to/python3. deps/setup.py checks this and stops with the same advice.\n\nThis installs FormationTemps.jl as a development package, meaning changes you make to the Julia source code are immediately reflected without reinstalling.","title":"From a Local Clone"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#From-the-Julia-Package-Registry","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"If you want to use the released version of FormationTemps.jl without cloning the repository:\n\npython -c \"import ssl; print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\"   # must be 3.5 or newer; see the note above\npip install juliacall juliapkg numpy matplotlib\n\nThen set the following environment variables in your shell profile (e.g. ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc) so they persist across sessions:\n\nexport PYTHON_JULIAPKG_EXE=$(which julia)\nexport JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1\n\nFinally, run the following once in Python to register the Julia dependency:\n\nimport juliapkg\njuliapkg.require_julia(\"~1.12\")\njuliapkg.add(\"FormationTemps\", \"03bcd87b-2230-4045-a5fa-95a5fcdd1ff8\", version=\"^3\")\njuliapkg.add(\"Korg\", \"acafc109-a718-429c-b0e5-afd7f8c7ae46\")\njuliapkg.resolve()\n\nThis pulls the latest compatible versions of FormationTemps.jl and Korg.jl from the Julia General registry. Korg is added explicitly because the examples call it directly, and Julia only loads packages the environment lists.\n\nwarning: Warning\nPYTHON_JULIAPKG_EXE is required on macOS with juliaup (see Troubleshooting below). JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1 prevents GC crashes in the PythonCall bridge — CPU multithreading is not available when calling from Python. The local clone setup script (deps/setup.py) handles both automatically.","title":"From the Julia Package Registry"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Basic-Usage","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"Once installed, FormationTemps.jl can be loaded in Python via:\n\nfrom juliacall import Main as jl\njl.seval(\"using Korg\")\njl.seval(\"using FormationTemps\")\nFT = jl.FormationTemps\nKorg = jl.Korg\n\nA complete example computing a formation temperature spectrum (following the Basic Tutorial) is shown below:\n\nimport os\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")   # interactive backends can crash under juliacall\n\nfrom juliacall import Main as jl\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# load FormationTemps\njl.seval(\"using Korg\")\njl.seval(\"using FormationTemps\")\nFT = jl.FormationTemps\nKorg = jl.Korg\n\n# apply project style; it renders text with LaTeX, so a TeX installation is required\nplt.style.use(os.path.join(str(FT.moddir), \"fig.mplstyle\"))\n\n# read the linelist (ships with the package)\n# slicing done on Julia side to preserve 1-based indexing\nlinelist = jl.seval(\n    'Korg.read_linelist(joinpath(FormationTemps.datdir, \"Sun_VALD.lin\"))[16000:16100]'\n)\n\n# set stellar parameters (velocities in m/s)\nstar = FT.StellarProps(Teff=5777.0, logg=4.44, Fe_H=0.0,\n                       vsini=2100.0, v_macro=3400.0, v_micro=850.0)\n\n# compute formation temperatures (use_gpu=False for portability)\n# `method` takes a Julia Symbol, which a Python str does not convert to\nresult = FT.calc_formation_temp(star, linelist, use_gpu=False,\n                                method=jl.Symbol(\"hirano\"), u1=0.43, u2=0.31)\n\n# extract results into numpy arrays\nwavs = np.asarray(result.wavs)\nflux = np.asarray(result.flux)\ntemps = np.asarray(result.form_temps)\n\n# plot\nfig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(9.6, 6.4), sharex=True)\nax1.plot(wavs, flux, c=\"k\")\nax1.set_ylabel(r\"{\\rm Normalized Flux}\")\nax2.plot(wavs, temps, c=\"k\")\nax2.set_xlabel(r\"{\\rm Vacuum Wavelength [\\AA]}\")\nax2.set_ylabel(r\"{\\rm Formation Temperature [K]}\")\nfig.tight_layout()\n\n# write out the file\noutpath = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \"formation_temps.png\")\nfig.savefig(outpath, dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nprint(f\"Saved {outpath}\")\nplt.close()\n\n\n","title":"Basic Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Troubleshooting","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"details: \\\"not a valid Julia executable\\\" on macOS\njuliaup installs Julia 1.12+ as .app bundles that juliapkg cannot find. The setup script handles this automatically for local clones. For registry installs, set PYTHON_JULIAPKG_EXE before importing juliacall:export PYTHON_JULIAPKG_EXE=$(which julia)\n\ndetails: \\\"julia compat entries have empty intersection\\\" (OpenSSL conflict)\nPython is linked against an OpenSSL older than 3.5. Check with python -c \"import ssl; print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)\" and see the note under From a Local Clone above.\n\ndetails: Julia GC crash (SIGBUS/segfault) during long computations\nJulia's multi-threaded garbage collector can conflict with PythonCall's runtime bridge, causing hard crashes. The setup script sets JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1 to avoid this. For registry installs, set it in your shell:export JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1This disables Julia's CPU multithreading but avoids the GC contention. GPU acceleration is unaffected — pass use_gpu=True to benefit from GPU parallelism even with a single Julia thread. If CPU multithreading is needed, run the computation in pure Julia and load the results in Python. See Parallelization for details.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"python/#Tips","page":"Python Tutorial","text":"tip: Performance\nThe first import juliacall is slow — Julia compiles code on first use. Subsequent calls in the same session are fast. Precompilation happens once per environment.\n\ntip: Getting speed without threads\nCPU multithreading is unavailable from Python (see above), which leaves two options. Reach for method=jl.Symbol(\"quadrature\") first: it solves the radiative transfer once per μ node instead of once per surface tile, so it recovers most of the cost of the missing threads without changing the physics — see Integration Methods. Beyond that, pass use_gpu=True if you have a CUDA-capable GPU configured with Julia's CUDA.jl; the two compose.\n\ntip: Type mapping between Python and Julia\nKeyword arguments map directly to Julia kwargs (e.g., FT.calc_formation_temp(star, linelist, use_gpu=False)). One exception: method takes a Julia Symbol, and a Python str does not convert to one — pass method=jl.Symbol(\"quadrature\") rather than method=\"quadrature\". Julia arrays can be converted to numpy with numpy.asarray(result.wavs), which is zero-copy for contiguous Float64 arrays. Python True/False map to Julia true/false automatically. Note that juliacall uses 0-based indexing from the Python side.\n\ntip: Matplotlib backend\nInteractive backends (e.g., macosx, Qt5Agg) can crash under juliacall due to event loop conflicts. Use matplotlib.use(\"Agg\") before importing pyplot and save figures to files instead of calling plt.show().","title":"Tips"},{"category":"section","location":"convolutions/#Broadening-and-Convolutions","page":"Broadening & Convolutions","text":"FormationTemps.jl exposes the broadening kernels and convolution routines it uses internally so they can be applied directly to any spectrum. This page demonstrates each option and compares the convolution approximation to full disk integration.\n\nSee the Public Functions page for full API documentation on each function.","title":"Broadening & Convolutions"},{"category":"section","location":"convolutions/#Disk-integration-vs.-convolution-approximation","page":"Broadening & Convolutions","text":"By default calc_formation_temp performs a numerical disk integration over the stellar surface (method=:disk). Passing method=:hirano (equivalently the legacy convolve=true) instead applies the Hirano et al. (2011) combined rotation + macroturbulence kernel in the Fourier domain. This is much faster, but ultimately an approximation. A third option, method=:quadrature, recovers most of the disk-integration accuracy at a fraction of the cost — see Integration Methods for the full comparison. The code below shows the disk-integration and Hirano modes and their difference:\n\n\n# --- compare disk integration vs. convolution approximation ---\n\n# disk integration (default, method=:disk)\nstar_props = FT.StellarProps(Teff=Teff, logg=logg, Fe_H=Fe_H,\n                             vsini=vsini, v_macro=ζ_RT, v_micro=ξ)\nresult_int = FT.calc_formation_temp(star_props, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:disk)\n\n# convolution approximation (method=:hirano uses the Hirano et al. 2011 kernel;\n# u1 and u2 are linear and quadratic limb-darkening coefficients)\nu1 = 0.43\nu2 = 0.31\nresult_conv = FT.calc_formation_temp(star_props, linelist; Δλ=0.01, method=:hirano, u1=u1, u2=u2)\n\nfig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(9.6, 7.2), sharex=true)\nax1.plot(wavs, result_int.flux,  label=\"{\\\\rm integration}\", lw=2.0, c=\"k\")\nax1.plot(wavs, result_conv.flux, label=\"{\\\\rm convolution}\", lw=2.0, ls=\"--\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Normalized Flux}\")\nax1.legend()\n\nax2.plot(wavs, result_int.flux .- result_conv.flux, c=\"k\", lw=0.8)\nax2.axhline(0, ls=\":\", c=\"gray\")\nax2.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Vacuum Wavelength [\\\\AA]}\")\nax2.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Integration} \\$-\\$ {\\\\rm Convolution}\")\nax2.set_xlim(5410, 5415)\n\nfig.tight_layout()\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"convolution_vs_integration.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: convolution vs integration)","title":"Disk integration vs. convolution approximation"},{"category":"section","location":"convolutions/#Broadening-kernels","page":"Broadening & Convolutions","text":"Three macroturbulence kernels are available. The Gray rotation kernel handles rotational broadening only; the isotropic and anisotropic radial-tangential (RT) kernels handle macroturbulent broadening. The Hirano kernel (used with method=:hirano) combines rotation and RT macro in a single Fourier-domain operation.\n\n\n# --- compare the broadening kernels directly ---\n\n# build a velocity grid for kernel visualization\nΔλ = wavs[2] - wavs[1]\nλ0 = wavs[length(wavs) ÷ 2 + 1]\nΔv = FT.c_ms * Δλ / λ0\nvs = collect((-length(wavs)÷2 : length(wavs)÷2 - 1)) .* Δv\n\n# evaluate the three macroturbulence kernels at disk center (μ=1)\nk_gray_rot  = FT.gray_rot_kernel(vs, vsini, u1)\nk_iso = FT.gray_iso_rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_RT)\nk_rt = FT.rt_macro_kernel(vs, ζ_RT, 1.0)\n\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6, 4.8))\nax1.plot(vs ./ 1e3, k_gray_rot, lw=2.0, label=\"{\\\\rm Gray rotation (vsini = $(round(Int, vsini/1e3)) km/s)}\")\nax1.plot(vs ./ 1e3, k_iso, lw=2.0, label=\"{\\\\rm Isotropic RT macro (}\\$\\\\zeta\\$ {\\\\rm = $(round(Int, ζ_RT/1e3)) km/s)}\")\nax1.plot(vs ./ 1e3, k_rt, lw=2.0, label=\"{\\\\rm Anisotropic RT macro at }\\$\\\\mu=1\\${\\\\rm  (}\\$\\\\zeta\\$ {\\\\rm = $(round(Int, ζ_RT/1e3)) km/s)}\")\nax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Velocity [km/s]}\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Kernel Amplitude}\")\nax1.set_xlim(-1.5*max(vsini, ζ_RT)/1e3, 1.5*max(vsini, ζ_RT)/1e3)\nax1.legend(fontsize=12)\nfig.tight_layout()\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"broadening_kernels.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: broadening kernels)","title":"Broadening kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"convolutions/#Applying-convolutions-to-a-spectrum","page":"Broadening & Convolutions","text":"Each kernel has a matching convolve_* function that accepts a wavelength grid and spectrum vector (or matrix):\n\n\n# --- apply the convolution functions to the base spectrum ---\n\nflux_gray_rot = FT.convolve_gray_rotation(wavs, flux_base, vsini, u1)\nflux_iso = FT.convolve_iso_rt_macro(wavs, flux_base, ζ_RT)\nflux_hirano = FT.convolve_hirano_rotmacro(wavs, flux_base, vsini, ζ_RT, u1, u2)\n\nfig, ax1 = plt.subplots(figsize=(9.6, 4.8))\nax1.plot(wavs, flux_base, label=\"{\\\\rm Unbroadened}\", lw=0.8)\nax1.plot(wavs, flux_gray_rot, label=\"{\\\\rm Gray rotation only}\", lw=2.0, ls=\"--\")\nax1.plot(wavs, flux_iso, label=\"{\\\\rm Isotropic RT macro only}\", lw=2.0, ls=\"-.\")\nax1.plot(wavs, flux_hirano, label=\"{\\\\rm Hirano rotation + macro}\", lw=2.0, ls=\":\")\nax1.set_xlim(5410, 5415)\nax1.set_xlabel(\"{\\\\rm Vacuum Wavelength [\\\\AA]}\")\nax1.set_ylabel(\"{\\\\rm Normalized Flux}\")\nax1.legend()\nfig.tight_layout()\nfname = joinpath(FT.moddir, \"docs\", \"src\", \"static\", \"broadened_spectra.png\")\nfig.savefig(fname, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\nplt.show()\nplt.clf(); plt.close()\n\n\n\n\n\n(Image: broadened spectra)","title":"Applying convolutions to a spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"convolutions/#Disk-integration-convergence","page":"Broadening & Convolutions","text":"The numerical disk integration approximates the stellar surface with an N_phi times 2N_phi grid of tiles. The plot below shows how the integrated flux converges toward the direct (no-integration) reference as the grid resolution increases. Both the mean and maximum absolute percent error across all wavelength bins are shown.\n\n(Image: disk integration convergence)\n\nAt N_phi = 128 (the default), the mean error is well below 1% and the maximum error is on the order of a few tenths of a percent. Doubling to N_phi = 256 or 512 reduces the error further but with diminishing returns and quadratically increasing cost, since the number of visible tiles scales as sim N_phi^2:\n\n(Image: cost vs Nphi)\n\nThe dotted line marks the cost of the absorption calculation (compute_alpha!), which is independent of N_phi. Below it there is nothing to gain from a coarser surface grid — the calculation is no longer tile-bound. For most applications N_phi = 128 provides a good balance between accuracy and runtime; if it is too slow, prefer method=:quadrature over coarsening N_phi, since it reduces the transfer cost rather than the fidelity of the surface grid.","title":"Disk integration convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Integration-Methods","page":"Integration Methods","text":"calc_formation_temp can integrate over the stellar disk in three ways, selected with the method keyword:\n\nmethod description worst-pixel error vs :disk cost\n:disk (default) explicit integration over Nϕ latitude bins reference seconds per call\n:quadrature Gauss–Legendre μ-quadrature, rotation applied per ring ≲ 2 K 18–28× less than :disk on CPU, 7–36× on GPU\n:hirano analytic rotation + macroturbulence kernel (needs u1, u2) tens of K once vsini ≳ 5 km/s 1.3–1.5× less than :quadrature on CPU, 1–2× on GPU\n\ncalc_formation_temp(star, linelist; method=:disk)                      # reference\ncalc_formation_temp(star, linelist; method=:quadrature)                # fast, near-reference\ncalc_formation_temp(star, linelist; method=:hirano, u1=0.43, u2=0.31)  # fastest, approximate\n\nAll three run on the GPU with use_gpu=true.\n\nnote: `convolve` is a deprecated alias\nconvolve=false maps to method=:disk and convolve=true to method=:hirano. New code should use method.","title":"Integration Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Which-method-to-use","page":"Integration Methods","text":":disk is the reference. It is the most accurate and the most expensive. Use it for the definitive answer, or to check the other two.\n:quadrature is the right choice almost everywhere else. It reproduces :disk, inclination and differential rotation included, to within about 2 K at the worst pixel and well under 0.1 K on average, for a twentieth to a thirtieth of the CPU time. gpu_precision=Float32 costs it no accuracy.\n:hirano trades a lot of accuracy for a little speed. It is only 1.3–1.5× faster than :quadrature on CPU, and on a GPU both finish in tens of milliseconds. Its error, tens of Kelvin once vsini ≳ 5 km/s, comes from its parametric limb darkening and does not shrink with resolution. Use it only when cost matters more than that.\n\n(Image: formation-temperature error vs vsini)\n\nWorst-pixel formation-temperature error against :disk. :quadrature is least accurate around vsini ≈ 1–2 km/s, where its rotation kernel spans only a few wavelength pixels.\n\n(Image: speed vs Nλ)\n\nWall-clock time per call (Nϕ = 128, Nμ = 32) on the machine described under Parallelization; error bars span repeated timings.","title":"Which method to use"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Tuning-:quadrature","page":"Integration Methods","text":"The defaults are converged, so there is rarely a reason to change them.\n\nNμ: more nodes than the default of 32 cost time without improving agreement.\n(Image: quadrature convergence)\nΔλ is the setting to refine for agreement better than about 0.3 K: the remaining error comes from placing the rotation kernel on the wavelength grid. :hirano does not improve with finer sampling.\n(Image: accuracy vs wavelength sampling)\nN_az only matters under differential rotation, and then by millikelvin. Leave it alone.\n\nThe figures are regenerated by benchmarks/benchmark_quadrature.jl followed by benchmarks/plot_quadrature.jl.","title":"Tuning :quadrature"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Rotation","page":"Integration Methods","text":"Rotation is set by vsini (the projected equatorial velocity), istar, and the differential-rotation coefficients α₂, α₄ on StellarProps. For rigid rotation istar has no effect. :hirano supports rigid rotation only.","title":"Rotation"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Caveats","page":"Integration Methods","text":"Saturated line cores. Where the contribution function still peaks at the top of the model atmosphere, the formation temperature is set by where the model was truncated and is a lower limit. boundary_mask flags those wavelengths; drop them before interpreting a formation-temperature spectrum:\nresult = calc_formation_temp(star, linelist)\ngood = .!boundary_mask(result)          # or boundary_mask(result; r_thresh=…)\nA line can also be wrong for reasons the contribution function does not show, such as LTE or bad atomic data; see Lines the model cannot get right.\nNarrow windows. If the rotation kernel is wider than the wavelength window, :quadrature warns that the broadening is truncated. Widen the window with minλ/maxλ.","title":"Caveats"}]
}
